from jsonb_array_elements('[["foundations","Human rights are best defined as:",["Standards granted to individuals by their State through national legislation, which the State may therefore withdraw","Standards that apply only in States that have adopted a democratic political system","Standards that belong only to the nationals and citizens of a State, to the exclusion of visitors and migrants","Universally agreed basic standards which belong to everyone from the mere fact of being human and which aim to ensure that every person is treated with dignity and respect"],3,null,"Rights are inherent: they belong to everyone by virtue of being human. The State does not give people their rights, it must respect, protect and fulfil them.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.A","fnd-001","concept"],["foundations","Which principle is described in the course as being ''at the centre of human rights'' and as featuring in all the major human rights treaties?",["The principle of proportionality","The principle of non-discrimination","The margin of appreciation","The principle of subsidiarity"],1,null,"Rights are inherent to all persons without discrimination and irrespective of the political system of the State. Subsidiarity, proportionality and the margin of appreciation are principles governing the Court''s work, not the foundation of human rights as such.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.A","fnd-002","concept"],["foundations","Under international law, States are obliged to respect, protect and fulfil human rights. ''To respect'' means:",["That States must compensate anyone whose rights have been violated","That States must take positive action to ensure the enjoyment of human rights","That States must pass laws and create mechanisms to prevent violations by State authorities and non-State actors","That States must not interfere with or restrict human rights"],3,null,"The triad is: respect = do not interfere; protect = legislate and create mechanisms against violations, including by private actors; fulfil = take positive action.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Role of the State ; Plan I.A","fnd-003","concept"],["foundations","Under international law, States are obliged to respect, protect and fulfil human rights. ''To protect'' means:",["Defending the State''s citizens before international courts","Refraining from any interference with the rights set out in the Convention","Taking positive action to ensure that everyone actually enjoys their rights","Passing laws and creating mechanisms to prevent violations of rights by State authorities AND by non-State actors"],3,null,"The protection obligation covers violations committed by private (non-State) actors as well, which is why purely ''vertical'' reasoning is insufficient.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Role of the State ; Plan I.A","fnd-004","concept"],["foundations","Under international law, States are obliged to respect, protect and fulfil human rights. ''To fulfil'' means:",["That States must execute the judgments of the European Court of Human Rights","That States must ratify every additional protocol to the Convention","That States must take positive action to ensure the enjoyment of human rights","That States must abstain from restricting human rights"],2,null,"''Fulfil'' is the proactive limb: creating the conditions in which rights become effective, not merely abstaining from violating them.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Role of the State ; Plan I.A","fnd-005","concept"],["foundations","The statement that human rights are ''interdependent and indivisible'' means that:",["There must be a violation of at least two rights for an application to be lodged with the Court","All the rights are intertwined, so that the fact of not respecting one of them may adversely affect another","Everyone is entitled to these rights in equal measure, without discrimination","Every right protected by the Convention has exactly the same legal regime"],1,null,"Interdependence and indivisibility describe the links between rights (non-observance of one may impact another). The wording about everyone being entitled to these rights in equal measure without discrimination describes universality and non-discrimination, a different idea. This is annales Question 5.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary ; QCM ANGLAIS Q5","fnd-006","concept"],["foundations","Which of the following is classified in the course as a civil and political right rather than an economic, social and cultural right?",["The right to education","The right to join a trade union","The right not to be subjected to torture","The right to an adequate standard of living"],2,null,"Civil and political: life, fair trial, freedom from torture. Economic, social and cultural: work, trade union, health, education, adequate standard of living.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.A","fnd-007","concept"],["foundations","Which of the following is classified in the course as an economic, social and cultural right?",["The right to a fair trial","The right to work","The right to life","The right not to be subjected to torture"],1,null,"Work, trade union membership, health, education and an adequate standard of living form the economic, social and cultural category.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.A","fnd-008","concept"],["systems","How many Member States does the Council of Europe currently have, according to the course?",["46","27","28","47"],0,null,"46 since Russia ceased to be a member in 2022. Careful: the printed course handout (an older Irish document) still says 47, but the lecture notes and the course plan say 46. 27 and 28 are European Union figures.",2,"The handout ''Guide for the Civil & Public Service'' still says 47. The correct current figure is 46.",null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","fnd-009","figure"],["systems","How many Member States does the European Union currently have, according to the course?",["46","28","47","27"],3,null,"27 since the United Kingdom left. 46 is the Council of Europe. The handout''s figure of 28 predates Brexit.",2,"The handout still says 28 (pre-Brexit).",null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","fnd-010","figure"],["systems","The primary human rights instrument of the Council of Europe is:",["The European Convention on Human Rights","The European Union Charter of Fundamental Rights","The Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action","The Universal Declaration of Human Rights"],0,null,"The Convention belongs to the Council of Europe. The Charter of Fundamental Rights belongs to the European Union.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","fnd-011","concept"],["systems","Rights are protected in the European Union by:",["The European Union Charter of Fundamental Rights, enforced by the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg","The European Convention on Human Rights, enforced by the European Court of Human Rights in Strasbourg","The European Convention on Human Rights, enforced by the Court of Justice of the European Union in Luxembourg","The European Union Charter of Fundamental Rights, enforced by the Court of Justice of the European Union in Luxembourg"],3,null,"Two distinct systems: Convention/Strasbourg for the Council of Europe, Charter/Luxembourg for the European Union.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","fnd-012","concept"],["systems","Which of the following statements about the relationship between the Council of Europe and the European Union is correct?",["The two organisations share the same structure and working methods but have different courts","The two organisations have identical membership","Every Member State of the European Union is also a Member State of the Council of Europe, but the reverse is not true","Every Member State of the Council of Europe is also a Member State of the European Union, but the reverse is not true"],2,null,"EU membership implies Council of Europe membership (46 vs 27). The two organisations are entirely separate in structure and working methods.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","fnd-013","concept"],["systems","Which of the following names is correct?",["European Convention OF Human Rights and European Court OF Human Rights","European Convention ON Human Rights and European Court ON Human Rights","European Convention OF Human Rights, but European Court ON Human Rights","European Convention ON Human Rights, but European Court OF Human Rights"],3,null,"A classic trap flagged by the lecturer: Convention ON, Court OF.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, opening ; Plan I.B (pièges de dénomination)","fnd-014","vocab"],["systems","What is the full official name of the ECHR?",["The European Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms","The European Charter for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms","The European Treaty on Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms","The European Convention for the Protection of Fundamental Rights and Public Freedoms"],0,null,"Usually shortened to ''European Convention on Human Rights''.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, opening ; Plan I.B","fnd-015","vocab"],["systems","The European Court of Human Rights sits in:",["Luxembourg","The Hague","Brussels","Strasbourg"],3,null,"Strasbourg for the European Court of Human Rights; Luxembourg for the Court of Justice of the European Union.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","fnd-016","concept"],["foundations","Where are human rights usually found in legal terms?",["Exclusively in United Nations treaties","In the case law of international courts alone, since no treaty can be exhaustive","In international or regional treaties, or in national constitutions and national legislation","Exclusively in the constitutions of democratic States"],2,null,"They form a legal statement of universally accepted principles on how a State should treat everyone within its jurisdiction.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.A","fnd-017","concept"],["foundations","According to the course, what is the classic shortcoming of most international and regional human rights instruments, which the ECHR avoids?",["The rights they contain remain theoretical because the instrument is a mere declaration of intent, with no court before which individuals can directly enforce them","They can be derogated from in times of war","They protect too few rights to be of practical use","They bind only the States that have ratified them"],0,null,"The ECHR is considered the most successful regional instrument precisely because its rights are directly enforceable against States and individuals have a right of individual application.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The European Convention on Human Rights ; Plan II","fnd-018","concept"],["convention","The European Convention on Human Rights was written in ... and came into force in ...",["1950 / 1959","1950 / 1953","1953 / 1959","1948 / 1950"],1,null,"Written 1950, in force 1953. 1948 is the Universal Declaration of Human Rights; 1959 is the year the Court itself was set up.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 2 ; Plan II","fnd-019","figure"],["convention","Article 1 of the Convention concerns:",["The right to an effective remedy","The obligation to respect human rights, requiring all parties to secure the Convention rights to everyone within their jurisdiction","The prohibition of discrimination","The right to life"],1,null,"Article 1 is the general obligation clause. Note that the Court sometimes expands the scope of the Convention by interpretation, finding rights not expressly stated.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 2 ; Plan II","fnd-020","article"],["convention","Who benefits from the rights contained in the Convention in a State party?",["Only the nationals and citizens of that State","Only nationals of States that are parties to the Convention","Everyone within the State''s jurisdiction, including visitors, migrant workers, asylum seekers and refugees","Only persons lawfully present on the territory of that State"],2,null,"Jurisdiction, not nationality, is the criterion. See Hirsi Jamaa and Others v Italy [2012], where migrants who were not Italian citizens were protected.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 1 ; Plan II","fnd-021","concept"],["convention","In Hirsi Jamaa and Others v Italy [2012], the Court held that:",["Convention rights apply even when a State operates outside its own territory, so migrants intercepted at sea and sent back to Libya fell within Italy''s jurisdiction","Italy could lawfully return the migrants because they were not Italian citizens","The case was inadmissible because the applicants had not exhausted Italian domestic remedies","Migrants intercepted on the high seas fall outside the jurisdiction of the intercepting State"],0,null,"The case illustrates the extraterritorial reach of the Article 1 jurisdiction obligation.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 1","fnd-022","case"],["foundations","The Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action of 1993 reaffirmed human rights as:",["Absolute, unqualified, inherent and inalienable","Universal, enforceable, justiciable and binding","Universal, indivisible, interdependent and interrelated","Civil, political, economic and social"],2,null,"Underpinned by further principles such as dignity, respect, transparency, accessibility and foreseeability.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), end of Section 5","fnd-023","concept"],["foundations","Which of the following is NOT listed in the course among the principles underpinning human rights alongside universality and indivisibility?",["Foreseeability","Dignity","Reciprocity","Accessibility"],2,null,"The course lists dignity, respect, transparency, accessibility and foreseeability. Reciprocity is alien to human rights logic: a State''s obligations do not depend on other States performing theirs.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), end of Section 5","fnd-024","concept"],["systems","The source document on which the course handout is based is:",["A textbook published by the University of Montpellier","The Council of Europe''s official commentary on the Convention","A guide published by the Registry of the European Court of Human Rights","''European Convention on Human Rights: Guide for the Civil & Public Service'', Irish Human Rights Commission"],3,null,"This explains why so many of the illustrative cases in the handout are Irish (Airey, Norris, Johnston, Keegan, DG).",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Source","fnd-025","concept"],["articles","Which Convention article protects the right to life?",["Article 2","Article 5","Article 1","Article 3"],0,null,"Art. 2 right to life; art. 3 prohibition of torture; art. 5 liberty and security; art. 1 general obligation.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-001","article"],["articles","Which Convention article prohibits torture and inhuman or degrading treatment?",["Article 4","Article 7","Article 2","Article 3"],3,null,"Article 3. The difference between torture and inhuman or degrading treatment is a matter of degree, assessed through the severity test.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-002","article"],["articles","Which Convention article prohibits slavery, servitude and forced labour?",["Article 3","Article 11","Article 4","Article 5"],2,null,"Article 4.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-003","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects the right to liberty and security, and therefore prohibits arbitrary detention?",["Article 4","Article 6","Article 2","Article 5"],3,null,"Article 5. Article 6 is the fair trial, which comes after the question of detention.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-004","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects the right to a fair trial?",["Article 5","Article 6","Article 13","Article 7"],1,null,"Article 6, the most frequently invoked article before the Court. Do not confuse it with article 13 (effective remedy before a national authority).",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-005","article"],["articles","Which Convention article lays down the principle ''no punishment without law''?",["Article 5","Article 6","Article 4","Article 7"],3,null,"Article 7, which prohibits retroactive criminal law and retroactive aggravation of penalties.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-006","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects the right to respect for private and family life?",["Article 8","Article 9","Article 12","Article 10"],0,null,"Article 8, a qualified right.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-007","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects freedom of thought, conscience and religion?",["Article 9","Article 10","Article 8","Article 11"],0,null,"Article 9. Article 10 is freedom of expression, article 11 freedom of assembly and association.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-008","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects freedom of expression?",["Article 10","Article 11","Article 8","Article 9"],0,null,"Article 10.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-009","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects freedom of assembly and association, including the right to form or join a trade union?",["Article 12","Article 11","Article 10","Article 9"],1,null,"Article 11.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-010","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects the right to marry?",["Article 14","Article 8","Article 11","Article 12"],3,null,"Article 12.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-011","article"],["articles","Which Convention article protects the right to an effective remedy?",["Article 6","Article 14","Article 13","Article 5"],2,null,"Article 13: the possibility to challenge a decision before a national authority, and that possibility must be effective.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-012","article"],["articles","Which Convention article prohibits discrimination?",["Article 12","Article 1","Article 14","Article 13"],2,null,"Article 14, which is not a standalone right and must be pleaded together with another Convention right.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, list of articles ; Plan II.A","art-013","article"],["articles","Which Convention article allows derogation in time of war or other public emergency?",["Article 1","Article 16","Article 14","Article 15"],3,null,"Article 15. Some rights may never be derogated from, including freedom from torture, freedom from slavery and no punishment without law.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 15","art-014","article"],["articles","Article 16 of the Convention deals with:",["Derogation in time of emergency","The obligation to furnish necessary facilities to the Court","The prohibition of the abuse of rights","Restrictions on the political activity of aliens"],3,null,"Article 16 concerns restrictions on the political activity of aliens. The course notes that there have been very few cases and no judgment to date finding a violation.",3,"The handout states that ''the government cannot restrict political activity simply because a person is not a citizen''. Article 16 in fact PERMITS such restrictions; it is the Court that reads the provision very narrowly (Piermont v France, 1995). Answer as taught, but do not repeat the handout''s gloss in an essay.",null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 16","art-015","article"],["protocols","The protection of private property is guaranteed by:",["Article 1 of the Convention","Article 1 of Protocol No. 7","Article 1 of Protocol No. 4","Article 1 of Protocol No. 1"],3,null,"Protocol 1, article 1. It does not mean that no one can be deprived of their property, but that deprivation must be provided for by law and, in principle, compensated.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-001","article"],["protocols","The right to education is guaranteed by:",["Article 14 of the Convention","Article 2 of Protocol No. 7","Article 3 of Protocol No. 1","Article 2 of Protocol No. 1"],3,null,"P1-2. It also requires the State to respect the right of parents to ensure education does not conflict with their religious beliefs, which is the basis of Lautsi v Italy.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Protocol 1 ; Plan II.B","prot-002","article"],["protocols","The right to free elections is guaranteed by:",["Article 3 of Protocol No. 1","Article 2 of Protocol No. 1","Article 5 of Protocol No. 7","Article 3 of Protocol No. 4"],0,null,"P1-3: States must hold free and secret elections.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-003","article"],["protocols","Which Protocol article abolishes the death penalty EXCEPT in specific circumstances such as times of war?",["Article 2 of the Convention","Article 1 of Protocol No. 6","Article 1 of Protocol No. 4","Article 1 of Protocol No. 13"],1,null,"P6-1 abolishes the death penalty with exceptions (war, treason). P13-1 abolishes it in ALL circumstances. This distinction is a classic trap.",3,"The course adds ''treason'' to the Protocol 6 exceptions. Article 2 of Protocol 6 in fact covers only acts committed in time of war or of imminent threat of war. Answer as taught, but know the text.",null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-004","article"],["protocols","Which Protocol article abolishes the death penalty in ALL circumstances?",["Article 1 of Protocol No. 13","Article 1 of Protocol No. 6","Article 2 of the Convention","Article 1 of Protocol No. 7"],0,null,"P13-1, with no exception for time of war. The course''s illustration of the ratification rule is Turkey and Protocol 13.",3,"The course says Turkey has not ratified Protocol 13. Turkey in fact ratified it on 20 February 2006 (in force 1 June 2006); today Azerbaijan is the only Council of Europe member not to have ratified it. The rule the example illustrates (a protocol binds only its ratifying States) remains correct.",null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-005","article"],["protocols","The right of appeal in criminal matters is guaranteed by:",["Article 2 of Protocol No. 7","Article 4 of Protocol No. 7","Article 3 of Protocol No. 7","Article 6 of the Convention"],0,null,"P7-2. P7-3 is compensation for wrongful conviction and P7-4 is ne bis in idem.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-006","article"],["protocols","Compensation for wrongful conviction is guaranteed by:",["Article 3 of Protocol No. 7","Article 2 of Protocol No. 7","Article 41 of the Convention","Article 4 of Protocol No. 7"],0,null,"P7-3. Article 41 is just satisfaction awarded by the Court itself, which is a different mechanism.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-007","article"],["protocols","The principle ''non bis in idem'', the right not to be tried or punished twice for the same facts, is guaranteed by:",["Article 7 of the Convention","Article 2 of Protocol No. 7","Article 4 of Protocol No. 7","Article 5 of Protocol No. 7"],2,null,"P7-4. Article 7 of the Convention deals with legality and non-retroactivity, which is a different matter.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-008","article"],["protocols","Equality between spouses is guaranteed by:",["Article 12 of the Convention","Article 5 of Protocol No. 7","Article 4 of Protocol No. 7","Article 14 of the Convention"],1,null,"P7-5. Article 12 is the right to marry, article 14 is the prohibition of discrimination in the enjoyment of other Convention rights.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Protocol 7","prot-009","article"],["protocols","Which Protocol contains the prohibition of imprisonment for debt, freedom of movement, the prohibition of expulsion of nationals and the prohibition of collective expulsion of aliens?",["Protocol No. 12","Protocol No. 4","Protocol No. 1","Protocol No. 7"],1,null,"Protocol 4, articles 1 to 4 respectively.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Protocol 4","prot-010","article"],["protocols","Protocol No. 12 concerns:",["The prohibition of collective expulsion of aliens","The right to free elections","A general prohibition of discrimination, extending protection beyond the enjoyment of the other Convention rights","The abolition of the death penalty in all circumstances"],2,null,"Whereas article 14 is not autonomous, Protocol 12 creates a free-standing prohibition of discrimination.",3,"The handout says Protocol 12 ''will enter into force when ratified by ten Contracting States''. It in fact entered into force on 1 April 2005, but only for the States that have ratified it.",null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Protocol 12 ; Article 14","prot-011","article"],["protocols","What is the key rule governing the scope of the additional protocols?",["A protocol binds every Member State of the Council of Europe as soon as it comes into force","A protocol binds only the States that voted in favour of it within the Committee of Ministers","A protocol binds a State as soon as that State has signed it, whether or not it has ratified it","A protocol binds only the States that have ratified it, whereas the Convention rights apply to all States parties"],3,null,"The course''s example: Turkey and Protocol 13, on the basis that a State that has not ratified is not bound by the total abolition of the death penalty.",2,"Careful: the example is out of date. Turkey ratified Protocol 13 in 2006. Azerbaijan is now the only member State not to have ratified it. The principle itself is unaffected.",null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","prot-012","concept"],["protocols","In the language of the Convention, a ''protocol'' is:",["A reservation entered by a State upon signature","An amendment or addition to a treaty or convention","A decision of the Committee of Ministers enforcing a judgment","A procedural rule governing hearings before the Court"],1,null,"Glossary definition. Protocols added new rights over time: private property, education, freedom of movement, free elections.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","prot-013","vocab"],["nature-of-rights","An ''absolute right'' is:",["A right that can be enforced exclusively by the European Court of Human Rights","A right which can be interfered with or derogated from under limited circumstances to protect specific public interests","A right regarding which no derogation is allowed","A right belonging only to the nationals of the Member States that did not opt out of articles 3, 4 and 7"],2,null,"Absolute (or unqualified) rights cannot be derogated from under any circumstances, not even in time of war. The wording about interference under limited circumstances to protect specific public interests is the definition of a QUALIFIED right, which is the trap. This is annales Question 6.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, The nature of rights ; Plan III ; QCM ANGLAIS Q6","nat-001","concept"],["nature-of-rights","A ''qualified right'' is:",["A right which may be interfered with or derogated from under limited circumstances in order to protect certain public interests, provided the interference is necessary and proportionate","A right which may only be invoked together with another Convention right","A right contained in a protocol rather than in the Convention itself","A right which no State may ever restrict"],0,null,"Beware of the wording about a right that may only be invoked together with another right: that describes article 14, which is non-autonomous, a different notion altogether.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, The nature of rights ; Plan III","nat-002","concept"],["nature-of-rights","According to the course plan, which articles are classified as absolute or unqualified rights?",["Articles 2, 3, 6 and 13","Articles 8, 10, 11 and Protocol 1 article 1","Articles 3, 5, 6 and 7","Articles 2, 3, 4 and 7"],3,null,"Right to life, prohibition of torture, prohibition of slavery, no punishment without law. Note the lecturer''s caveat: article 2 is presented as absolute even though the text itself provides for exceptions.",2,"The printed handout lists only articles 3, 4(1) and 7 as absolute. The lecture notes and the course plan add article 2. Follow the plan: 2, 3, 4, 7.",null,"CM Anglais, The nature of rights ; Plan III","nat-003","concept"],["nature-of-rights","According to the course plan, which of the following sets are qualified rights?",["Articles 5, 6, 13 and 14","Articles 8, 10, 11 and Protocol 1 article 1","Articles 2, 3, 4 and 7","Articles 1, 12, 15 and 16"],1,null,"Private and family life, freedom of expression, freedom of assembly, protection of property. The handout also includes article 9 in this list.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, The nature of rights ; Plan III","nat-004","concept"],["nature-of-rights","The ''two-tier test'' applied by the Court to a State''s interference with a qualified right consists in asking:",["First, whether the right at issue is absolute; second, whether the State has ratified the relevant protocol","First, whether the interference serves a legitimate aim; second, whether it was necessary and proportionate","First, whether the interference was provided for by law; second, whether compensation was paid","First, whether domestic remedies were exhausted; second, whether the application was lodged in time"],1,null,"Worked example from the lecture: deporting an undocumented foreign national always pursues a legitimate aim (fighting illegal immigration), but proportionality depends on the strength of the person''s family ties in the country.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The nature of rights ; Plan III","nat-005","concept"],["nature-of-rights","Regarding the protection of Convention rights, the notion of ''proportionality'' refers to:",["The fact that it is never possible for a State to interfere with any right protected by the Convention, since rights are inherent to everyone","The possibility for a State to interfere with one or several QUALIFIED rights provided that the interference is necessary and proportionate to a legitimate aim","The possibility for a State to interfere with one or several ABSOLUTE rights provided that the interference is necessary and proportionate to a legitimate aim","The possibility for a State to interfere with any Convention right, whether qualified or absolute, provided that the interference is necessary and proportionate to a legitimate aim"],1,null,"Proportionality applies to qualified rights only. There is no proportionality analysis for an absolute right: any interference is a violation. This is annales Question 8.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of proportionality ; Plan V.A ; QCM ANGLAIS Q8","nat-006","concept"],["nature-of-rights","Under article 15 of the Convention, which rights may NEVER be derogated from even in time of war or other public emergency?",["Freedom of expression, freedom of assembly and freedom of religion","Freedom from torture, freedom from slavery and no punishment without law","All the rights contained in the Convention and its protocols","The right to a fair trial, the right to liberty and the right to an effective remedy"],1,null,"Articles 3, 4 and 7 in the handout''s list. Derogation also requires a real public emergency threatening the life of the nation and that the State set out the rights it intends to limit and the oversight mechanisms envisaged.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 15","nat-007","article"],["nature-of-rights","In the glossary of the course, ''derogation or reservation'' means:",["The withdrawal of a State from the Council of Europe","The refusal of a State to execute a judgment of the Court","A decision by the Court not to examine an application on the merits","Non-application or non-acceptance of part of a treaty or convention, usually for a limited period of time"],3,null,"Glossary definition.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","nat-008","vocab"],["article-2","Article 2 imposes on the State:",["An obligation to refrain from intentionally and unlawfully taking someone''s life AND an obligation to safeguard the lives of people within its jurisdiction","Only an obligation to prosecute those who unlawfully take a life","An obligation to abolish the death penalty in all circumstances","Only an obligation to refrain from intentionally and unlawfully taking someone''s life"],0,null,"Article 2 has a negative limb (do not kill) and a positive limb (introduce laws and criminal penalties protecting life). Abolition of the death penalty comes from Protocols 6 and 13, not from article 2.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article II ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 2","a2-001","article"],["article-2","Under article 2, deprivation of life does NOT contravene the Convention when it results from the use of force which is no more than absolutely necessary. Which of the following is NOT one of the situations listed?",["In defence of any person from unlawful violence","In order to carry out a death sentence lawfully imposed by a court","In order to effect a lawful arrest or prevent the escape of a person lawfully detained","When acting lawfully to prevent a riot"],1,null,"The three listed situations are: defence against unlawful violence, lawful arrest or prevention of escape, and quelling a riot. Execution of a death sentence is dealt with by Protocols 6 and 13, not by this list.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article II ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 2","a2-002","article"],["article-2","Where a suspicious death occurs, article 2 has been interpreted as requiring the State to:",["Investigate the death properly","Refer the matter to the European Court of Human Rights","Compensate the family of the deceased automatically","Prosecute the person suspected of causing the death"],0,null,"This is the procedural limb of article 2.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article II ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 2","a2-003","concept"],["article-2","In Kontrová v Slovakia [2007], the Court found a violation of article 2 because:",["Slovak police officers had themselves used lethal force against the applicant''s children","The police had failed to pursue reported incidents of domestic violence and thereby failed to take appropriate positive action to protect the lives of the applicant''s children, who were later murdered by her husband","Slovakia had failed to investigate the deaths after they occurred","Slovakia had not made domestic violence a criminal offence"],1,null,"The case is the course''s illustration of positive obligations under article 2: the State''s liability arises from the authorities'' inaction.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article II ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 2","a2-004","case"],["article-3","Article 3 provides that no one shall be subjected to torture or to inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment. This prohibition:",["Is absolute and may not be derogated from, even in times of war or public emergency","May be restricted where necessary in the interests of national security","May be derogated from in times of war or public emergency threatening the life of the nation","Applies only to acts committed by State agents, never to omissions"],0,null,"Article 3 is the archetypal absolute right, and it can be violated by omission as well (see Z v UK).",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 3","a3-001","article"],["article-3","How does the Court determine whether conduct amounts to torture, to inhuman treatment or to degrading treatment?",["It applies the margin of appreciation, leaving each State to define the three notions","It applies a proportionality test, weighing the treatment against the aim pursued by the authorities","It relies on the classification adopted by the domestic courts of the respondent State","It applies a ''severity test'': ill-treatment must pass a minimum level of severity, and the distinction between the three is a matter of degree"],3,null,"The difference between torture and inhuman or degrading treatment is one of degree, assessed under the severity test. No proportionality reasoning applies: article 3 is absolute.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 3","a3-002","concept"],["article-3","In Ireland v UK [1978], the Court held that the five sensory deprivation and disorientation techniques used by the British authorities in Northern Ireland amounted to:",["A violation of article 5 only, the detention itself being unlawful","No violation of article 3, since the persons detained were suspected IRA members","Inhuman and degrading treatment, but not torture, as they were not at the time considered severe enough","Torture"],2,null,"A violation of article 3 was found against the UK, but the treatment was classified as inhuman and degrading rather than torture. This is annales Question 12, where the correct answer is precisely ''inhuman and degrading treatment''.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III ; QCM ANGLAIS Q12","a3-003","case"],["article-3","What made Ireland v UK [1978] procedurally remarkable?",["It was the first case in which the Court awarded just satisfaction","It was the first case in which a State was ordered to amend its Constitution","It was the first case decided by the Grand Chamber","It was the first inter-State case brought before the European Court of Human Rights"],3,null,"Inter-State cases are rare but usually significant. Note that a State may bring a case against another State, which disposes of the annales Question 12 distractor claiming that inter-State applications are impossible.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 3","a3-004","case"],["article-3","Which techniques were at issue in Ireland v UK [1978]?",["Prolonged solitary confinement in an unlit cell","Sleep deprivation, exposure to permanent noise, and withholding of food and drink","Electric shocks and beatings during interrogation","Forced labour in a high-security prison"],1,null,"Five sensory deprivation and disorientation techniques used in places of detention in Northern Ireland against IRA members.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 3","a3-005","case"],["article-3","In Z v UK [2001], the Court found a violation of article 3 because:",["A Local Authority had failed to remove four children from their mother even though it was aware that they were subjected to an unacceptable level of abuse and neglect over four years","The complexity of High Court procedure had made self-representation ineffective in practice","A journalist had been fined for contempt of court for refusing to reveal the identity of a whistleblower","Two soldiers had been investigated and dismissed from the armed forces because of their homosexuality"],0,null,"Z v UK is the course''s illustration that article 3 can be violated by omission. This scenario is annales Question 17.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III ; QCM ANGLAIS Q17","a3-006","case"],["article-4","Which of the following is NOT one of the four types of work excluded from the notion of forced or compulsory labour under article 4?",["Work done during legitimate detention or on conditional release","Work performed under an ordinary employment contract at below the minimum wage","Compulsory military service","Community service in a public emergency"],1,null,"The four exclusions are: work during legitimate detention or conditional release, compulsory military service, community service in a public emergency, and any work forming part of normal civic obligations.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article IV ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 4","a4-001","article"],["article-4","In the course example, doctors and pharmacists required by their professional body to be on duty at night and at weekends argued this amounted to forced labour. The Court:",["Found a violation of article 4 combined with article 14","Dismissed the case, holding that this was work forming part of a normal civic obligation","Found a violation of article 4, since the obligation was imposed without consent","Held the application inadmissible because professional obligations fall outside the Convention"],1,null,"''Normal civic obligation'' is the fourth exclusion listed by article 4.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article IV","a4-002","concept"],["article-4","In Siliadin v France [2005], the Court found a violation of article 4 because:",["France had required the applicant to perform community service in a public emergency","France had itself subjected the applicant to forced labour through a public authority","French criminal legislation at the time had failed to protect a 15-year-old girl, brought from Togo and made to work 15 hours a day, seven days a week, without pay, from servitude","France had refused to grant the applicant a residence permit"],2,null,"The violation lay in the inadequacy of the criminal law to protect her, a positive obligation, and the perpetrators were private individuals.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 4","a4-003","case"],["article-5","Article 5 provides that an arrest or detention is lawful only if it falls within one of:",["Six permitted grounds exhaustively listed","An open list of grounds, provided the detention is proportionate","Three permitted grounds exhaustively listed","Four permitted grounds exhaustively listed"],0,null,"Six grounds. This figure is very QCM-friendly.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article V ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 5","a5-001","article"],["article-5","Which of the following is NOT one of the six permitted grounds of detention under article 5?",["Conviction by a competent criminal court","Failure to obey a court order","To prevent unauthorised entry into a country of a person who has been deported or extradited","To prevent a person from expressing opinions that threaten public order"],3,null,"The six grounds: conviction by a criminal court; failure to obey a court order; ensuring attendance at court where there is reasonable suspicion of an offence; educational supervision of a minor or to bring a minor before a court; persons of unsound mind, alcoholics, addicts, vagrants or carriers of infectious diseases; preventing unauthorised entry or securing deportation or extradition.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article V ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 5","a5-002","article"],["article-5","Under article 5, a person may be detained on the ground of unsound mind, alcoholism or addiction, or:",["Because they have refused medical treatment","Because they have refused to pay a private debt","Because they are a foreign national without documentation","Because they carry an infectious disease"],3,null,"The ground covers persons of unsound mind, alcoholics, addicts, vagrants and carriers of infectious diseases. The course notes the British authorities'' use of this ground against people with AIDS in the 1980s. Note that the prohibition of imprisonment for debt is a separate matter, dealt with by Protocol 4 article 1.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article V ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 5","a5-003","article"],["article-5","Even where an arrest or detention falls within one of the six permitted grounds, the Court may still find a violation of article 5 if:",["The detention lasted more than six months","The person detained is a foreign national","The person was not assisted by a lawyer during the arrest","The deprivation of liberty is considered unreasonable"],3,null,"Lawful ground and reasonableness are cumulative requirements, as DG v Ireland illustrates.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article V ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 5","a5-004","concept"],["article-5","In DG v Ireland [2002], the Court found a violation of article 5 because:",["A minor had been detained solely because he was considered a danger to himself","A minor had been detained without ever being brought before a court","A minor had been detained for longer than the maximum period permitted by Irish law","A minor who should have been placed in a secure educational facility was detained in an institution for young offenders, which is not lawful detention for the purposes of educational supervision"],3,null,"The ground itself (educational supervision of a minor) was available, but the place of detention did not match it, so the detention was outside the article.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article V ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 5","a5-005","case"],["article-6","Which Convention article generates more cases before the European Court of Human Rights than any other?",["Article 8","Article 13","Article 6","Article 3"],2,null,"The right to a fair trial, in both criminal and civil cases.",1,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 6","a6-001","concept"],["article-6","Article 6 entitles everyone to:",["A hearing within six months of the charge being brought","A fair and public hearing before a court composed exclusively of professional judges","A fair and public hearing within a reasonable time by an independent and impartial body established by law","A hearing before a court of the same nationality as the accused"],2,null,"Independence, impartiality, a legal basis, publicity and reasonable time are the structural requirements.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 6","a6-002","article"],["article-6","Which of the following is NOT among the minimum rights of a person charged with a criminal offence under article 6?",["The right to be informed promptly of the charges in a language he or she understands","The right to be released on bail pending trial","The right to have time and facilities to prepare a defence","The right to have witnesses examined"],1,null,"The minimum rights listed are: information on the charges in an understandable language, time and facilities to prepare a defence, self-defence or legal assistance, examination of witnesses, and an interpreter if required. Pre-trial release is dealt with under article 5.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 6","a6-003","article"],["article-6","Why did the Court hold that the French ''procureur de la République'' is not an independent authority for the purposes of article 6?",["Because he is appointed by the Conseil supérieur de la magistrature","Because he represents the interests of the victim","Because he takes orders from the government, which is why the decision to extend police custody now falls to the juge des libertés et de la détention","Because he is not a professional judge"],2,null,"A textbook example of the Convention''s direct effect on French criminal procedure.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI ; Plan IV","a6-004","concept"],["article-6","In Airey v Ireland [1979], the Court found a violation of article 6 because:",["Legal aid was available only in criminal cases, so the applicant could not afford the lawyer she needed in practice to obtain a judicial separation, leaving her without effective access to a court","The Irish courts had refused to grant the applicant a divorce","The applicant had been denied the assistance of an interpreter","The proceedings had lasted an unreasonably long time"],0,null,"A right that exists only on paper is not an effective right. Ireland introduced civil legal aid as a result. Careful: annales Question 18 uses this very scenario as a distractor, the right answer there being Open Door and not Airey.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 6","a6-005","case"],["article-6","In Tsfayo v UK, the Court found a violation of article 6-1 because:",["The Housing Benefits Review Board that heard the applicant''s appeal was not independent of the Council whose decision was challenged, nor impartial in its processes","The applicant, an Ethiopian national, had been discriminated against on grounds of nationality","The proceedings before the High Court had lasted more than five years","The applicant had been denied legal aid"],0,null,"The structural point is the same as in Chahal under article 13: the reviewing body must be independent of the body whose decision is challenged.",3,"The handout dates this case 2007; the judgment was in fact delivered on 14 November 2006.",null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 6","a6-006","case"],["article-6","The course explains that French law was changed to allow the assistance of a lawyer in police custody following a judgment delivered against:",["Turkey, whose legislation was the same as French law at the time","Belgium","The United Kingdom","France itself"],0,null,"This is the course''s key illustration of why one must rely on the Court''s case law against other States, not only against the respondent State.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI and Subsidiarity ; Plan IV and VI.A","a6-007","case"],["article-7","The principle of ''no punishment without law'' under article 7:",["Prevents a State from convicting someone of a crime for an act that was not a crime at the time it was committed","Prevents a State from enacting civil or criminal laws that would have any retroactive effect","Prevents a State from increasing OR reducing a penalty retrospectively","Prevents a State from sentencing a person who was refused legal assistance during police questioning"],0,null,"Three traps here. Article 7 concerns criminal law, not civil law; a retroactive REDUCTION of the penalty is permitted (rétroactivité in mitius); and the lawyer in custody is an article 6 issue. This is annales Question 10.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VII ; Plan IV ; QCM ANGLAIS Q10","a7-001","article"],["article-7","A person commits an offence at a time when the maximum sentence is two years. Before trial, the law is changed and the maximum becomes three years. Under article 7:",["Only the old law can be applied, so the maximum remains two years","The new law applies only if the accused consents","The court may choose between the two laws","The new law applies, so the maximum becomes three years"],0,null,"The reverse is permitted: if the new maximum were one year, the more lenient new law could be applied. That is the retroactivity in mitius the course insists on.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VII ; Plan IV","a7-002","concept"],["article-7","Article 7 is also said to ensure the principle of legal certainty, which means that:",["The law must be published in the official journal before it enters into force","Criminal proceedings must be concluded within a reasonable time","The law can be used as a basis for punishability only if it clearly describes the criminal behaviour and its consequences","A person may not be tried twice for the same facts"],2,null,"The clearer the definition of the offence, the less scope for divergent interpretations. Non bis in idem belongs to Protocol 7 article 4.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 7","a7-003","concept"],["article-7","In Kononov v Latvia [2010], the Court held that the applicant''s conviction did NOT violate article 7 because:",["The acts of which he was convicted constituted a crime under international law at the time they were committed, even though they were not an offence in Latvian domestic law","Latvia had entered a reservation to article 7","War crimes are excluded from the scope of article 7","The applicant had waived his article 7 rights by pleading guilty"],0,null,"Acts committed as a soldier in the Soviet Army during the Second World War. The reference point for legality under article 7 is not only domestic law.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 7","a7-004","case"],["article-8","Under article 8, an interference by a public authority with the right to respect for private and family life is permissible where:",["The interference is in accordance with the law, whether or not it pursues a legitimate aim","Never: article 8 admits of no interference whatsoever","The interference pursues one of the aims listed in the article, whether or not it is proportionate","The interference is in accordance with the law, pursues one of the aims listed in the article, and is proportionate"],3,null,"Article 8 is a qualified right: legality, legitimate aim AND proportionality are cumulative. Any answer dropping proportionality is wrong. Annales Question 7 is built on exactly this point.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VIII ; Plan IV ; QCM ANGLAIS Q7","a8-001","article"],["article-8","Which of the following is NOT one of the grounds on which article 8 may be restricted?",["To protect health or morals","To preserve the cultural identity of the nation","To prevent disorder or crime","For the economic well-being of the country"],1,null,"The six listed grounds are: national security, public safety, the economic well-being of the country, the prevention of disorder or crime, the protection of health or morals, and the protection of the rights and freedoms of others.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VIII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 8 ; Plan IV","a8-002","article"],["article-8","Article 8 has also been interpreted as meaning that:",["The State must compensate any interference with private life, even a proportionate one","Every interference with private life must be authorised by a judge beforehand","Where decisions are taken which will affect a person''s privacy or family life, that person must generally be involved in the decision-making process","Private life is protected only within the home"],2,null,"A procedural dimension read into a substantive right.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 8","a8-003","concept"],["article-8","The case brought against the United Kingdom by Jeff Dudgeon established that:",["The dismissal of homosexual soldiers from the armed forces violates article 8","Homosexuality falls within the scope of private life, which reversed the earlier position under which laws prohibiting homosexuality were justified by the protection of health and morals","Homosexual couples have a right to marry under article 12","Discrimination on grounds of sexual orientation is prohibited by article 14 alone"],1,null,"This decriminalised homosexuality in Northern Ireland (England and Wales had already done so in 1967); Ireland followed with Norris (1988) and Cyprus with Modinos.",2,"Course materials date Dudgeon 1982; the judgment was delivered on 22 October 1981. The handout also dates Modinos v Cyprus 1994, whereas it was 22 April 1993, and Kokkinakis 1994, whereas it was 25 May 1993.",null,"CM Anglais, Article VIII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 8 ; Plan IV","a8-004","case"],["article-8","Fadeyeva v Russia [2005] shows that article 8:",["Protects Travellers from summary eviction from local authority sites","Has been applied in environmental matters, imposing an obligation on the State to protect individuals from the impact of living near a polluting industry","Protects the confidentiality of medical data transferred between public authorities","Protects a father''s right to be involved in the adoption process of his child"],1,null,"Article 8 has been read as imposing an obligation on the State to protect individuals from the impact of living near a polluting industry. Do not confuse it with the transfer of confidential medical data (MS v Sweden), the summary eviction of a Traveller family (Connors v UK) or a father''s involvement in the adoption of his child (Keegan v Ireland).",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 8","a8-005","case"],["article-8","In Connors v UK [2004], the Court found a violation of article 8 because:",["A Traveller family was refused permission to settle on a site of their choosing","A Traveller family was evicted from a local authority site by a summary procedure, with no justification offered and no possibility of challenge before the courts","The applicant was denied social housing on grounds of ethnicity","The applicant''s home was searched without a warrant"],1,null,"The Court held that the position of Travellers as a minority requires special consideration of their needs and way of life, both in the regulatory framework and in decision-making.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 8","a8-006","case"],["article-8","According to the course, the notion of ''family life'' under article 8 is broad and includes:",["Only relationships involving a minor child","The relationship between an unmarried couple, and a father''s right to be involved in the adoption process of his child","Only relationships recognised as such by the domestic law of the respondent State","Only relationships between married spouses and their legitimate children"],1,null,"Johnston v Ireland for the unmarried couple, Keegan v Ireland (1994) for the father and the adoption process.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 8","a8-007","case"],["article-9","Article 9 is structured in two parts. The second part provides that freedom of thought, conscience and religion:",["May be restricted only in very limited circumstances, such as in the interests of public safety or to protect the rights and freedoms of others","May be restricted only in time of war or public emergency","May be restricted at the discretion of each State under its margin of appreciation","May never be restricted, since it is an absolute right"],0,null,"Both article 9 and article 10 share this two-part structure: the right, then the permitted restrictions.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article IX ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 9 ; Plan IV","a9-001","article"],["article-9","Kokkinakis v Greece is remarkable because:",["It was the first inter-State case concerning freedom of religion","It was the first case brought by a religious organisation rather than an individual","It was the first time the Court found a violation of article 9, in the case of a Jehovah''s Witness convicted for proselytism","It was the first case in which the Court applied the margin of appreciation"],2,null,"Greece could not show that the conviction was justified in the interests of public safety.",3,"The handout dates this case 1994; the judgment was delivered on 25 May 1993.",null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 9","a9-002","case"],["article-10","Which of the following is a ground on which freedom of expression may be restricted under article 10, according to the course?",["Preserving the economic well-being of the country","Protecting national cultural heritage","Protecting the reputation of the government","Preventing the disclosure of information received in confidence"],3,null,"The listed grounds are public safety, the prevention of crime, the protection of health or morals, the protection of the rights of others, and preventing the disclosure of information received in confidence. The last of these is, as the lecturer notes, a protection for lawyers. Beware: ''economic well-being of the country'' belongs to article 8, not article 10.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article X ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 10 ; Plan IV","a10-001","article"],["article-10","In Open Door Counselling Ltd and Dublin Well Woman Centre v Ireland [1992], the Court held that Ireland had violated article 10 because:",["Ireland had prohibited abortion altogether","Ireland had prosecuted the applicants for contempt of court","An injunction prevented the applicants from providing information on abortion services legally available in other countries, which was disproportionate to the aims pursued","Ireland had denied the applicants access to a court to challenge the injunction"],2,null,"The Court''s reasoning is proportionality-based: Ireland could limit the information, but not prevent it entirely. This is annales Question 18.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article X ; QCM ANGLAIS Q18","a10-002","case"],["article-11","Article 11 protects the right to assemble with others:",["Provided the assembly is peaceful, and the right is balanced against the rights of others who wish to use the same space","Only where the assembly is organised by a registered association or trade union","Only where prior authorisation has been obtained from the authorities","In all circumstances, including where the assembly turns violent"],0,null,"The right entitles people to attend and organise marches so long as they remain peaceful.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XI ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 11","a11-001","article"],["article-11","In Wilson and the National Union of Journalists and Others v UK [2002], the Court found a violation of article 11 because:",["The UK had banned a trade union on grounds of national security","UK law allowed employers to use financial incentives to encourage employees to surrender their trade union rights","The applicant had been dismissed for belonging to a trade union","The UK had refused to recognise a right to strike"],1,null,"The employer had offered a 4.5% pay rise to staff signing contracts giving up collective bargaining and union recognition; Mr Wilson refused and was denied the rise.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 11","a11-002","case"],["article-12","Under article 12, national authorities have some flexibility to place limitations on marriage, such as setting the marriageable age, provided that:",["Those rules are identical in all Member States","Those rules never impair the essence of the right to marry","Those rules are approved by the Committee of Ministers","Those rules apply equally to nationals and foreigners"],1,null,"Regulation is allowed, negation is not.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 12 ; Plan IV","a12-001","article"],["article-12","In Johnston v Ireland, the Court held that:",["There was a violation of article 12 and of article 8 in respect of both applicants","There was a violation of article 12, because the prohibition of divorce made the right to marry ineffective","There was no violation of article 12, because the right to marry does not include the right to divorce, but there was a violation of article 8 on behalf of the couple''s daughter, since Irish law failed to respect the family life of a child born outside marriage","The application was inadmissible because divorce falls outside the scope of the Convention"],2,null,"A double-barrelled holding that is ideal QCM material: no violation of article 12, violation of article 8 on account of the daughter''s status. Strictly, the Court found the article 8 violation in respect of all three applicants, because of the legal situation of the child born outside marriage.",2,"Course materials date this case both 1986 and 1987. The judgment was delivered on 18 December 1986.",null,"CM Anglais, Article XII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 12 ; Plan IV","a12-002","case"],["article-13","For the purposes of article 13, a remedy is ''effective'' only if:",["The authority deciding on the remedy is independent of the body said to have violated the right in the first place, and decides within a reasonable time","The remedy is available before a court rather than an administrative body","The remedy suspends the execution of the contested decision in every case","The remedy results in compensation being paid to the applicant"],0,null,"The mere theoretical possibility of challenging a decision is not enough; the review must be genuine and independent.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 13 ; Plan IV","a13-001","article"],["article-13","In Chahal v UK [1996], the Court found a violation of article 13 because:",["The UK had refused to grant the applicant legal aid for his appeal","The applicant was deported before his appeal had been heard","The advisory panel that considered the applicant''s appeal against deportation did not offer a fully independent examination of whether his article 3 rights would be at risk if he were deported to India","The applicant had no right of appeal at all against the deportation order"],2,null,"Mr Chahal was a Sikh separatist due to be deported on national security grounds. Note the articulation: the article 13 violation concerns the review of an alleged article 3 risk.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 13 ; Plan IV","a13-002","case"],["article-14","Article 14 is described as ''not a standalone right''. This means that:",["It may only be invoked once all the other Convention arguments have failed","It may only be invoked by a State against another State","It applies only to discrimination by private individuals, not by the State","The alleged discrimination must fall within the scope of another provision of the Convention, and it must be pleaded together with that other right"],3,null,"Protocol 12 would extend this protection into a general, free-standing prohibition of discrimination.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIV ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 14 ; Plan IV","a14-001","article"],["article-14","The expression ''or other status'' in article 14 has been interpreted by the Court as covering:",["Only economic and social grounds such as property and income","Disability, health status and sexual orientation","Only grounds recognised as such by the domestic law of the respondent State","Nothing beyond the grounds expressly listed, which form an exhaustive list"],1,null,"The open-ended formula broadens the reach of the article well beyond sex, race, colour, language, religion, opinion, origin, minority association, property and birth.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIV ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 14 ; Plan IV","a14-002","article"],["article-14","In addition to showing that the case falls within the scope of another Convention provision, what must an applicant relying on article 14 demonstrate?",["That they belong to a recognised national minority","That the discrimination was intentional on the part of the State","That the same discrimination has already been condemned by the Court against another State","That they are treated less favourably than others in relation to that right"],3,null,"Comparative less favourable treatment is the core of the test.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 14","a14-003","concept"],["article-14","In 2000, the Court found a violation of article 14 by France on account of the law then applicable in the field of:",["Social security law","Inheritance law","Employment law","Property law"],1,null,"The French rule under which a child born of adultery received only half the share of the other children in a succession. France amended its legislation as a result. This is annales Question 16; the case is Mazurek v France, 1 February 2000.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIV ; Plan IV ; QCM ANGLAIS Q16","a14-004","case"],["article-14","The Belgian Linguistics Case [1968] is significant because:",["It was the first inter-State case before the Court","It was the first article 14 case brought before the Court and the first on the right to education, the Court finding a violation of Protocol 1 article 2 in conjunction with article 14","It was the first case in which the Court found a violation of article 14 taken alone","It was the first case in which a State was ordered to change its language legislation"],1,null,"French-speaking children were denied access to French-speaking schools solely on the basis of their parents'' addresses. Note the combination technique: P1-2 taken with article 14.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 14","a14-005","case"],["article-14","In Larkos v Cyprus [1999], the Court found violations of:",["Article 8 and article 14, holding that the State should offer the same protection to all types of tenants","Article 14 taken alone","Protocol 1 article 1 alone","Article 6 and article 13"],0,null,"A local authority tenant had less security of tenure than a tenant of a private landlord.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Equality and Non-Discrimination","a14-006","case"],["protocol-1-1","The article dealing with the protection of property:",["Allows a State to deprive someone of their property without any compensation if it is in the public interest","Allows a State to deprive someone of their property even if it is not in the public interest, subject to the payment of due compensation","Prevents a State from depriving someone of their property even if it is in the public interest","Prevents a State from depriving someone of their property, except if it is in the public interest and subject to the payment of due compensation"],3,null,"Public interest AND compensation are cumulative. This is annales Question 11.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Protocole 1 article 1 ; QCM ANGLAIS Q11","p11-001","article"],["protocol-1-1","Protocol 1 article 1 is divided into three parts. Which of the following is the third?",["Everyone is entitled to compensation where their property is expropriated","No one may have their possessions taken away except as provided for by law","The provisions may not restrict the State in enforcing laws necessary to control the use of property or to secure the payment of taxes or other charges","Everyone is entitled to the peaceful enjoyment of their possessions"],2,null,"The third limb is what saves tax measures, as the course''s example of the tax on vacant dwellings in Montpellier illustrates.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Protocole 1 article 1 ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Protocol 1 Article 1 ; Plan IV","p11-002","article"],["protocol-1-1","An owner leaves a flat empty in a city under housing pressure and is taxed on the vacant dwelling. He complains of a violation of Protocol 1 article 1. According to the course, the Court''s answer is that:",["There is a violation, because the owner may do as he wishes with what he owns","The complaint is inadmissible, because taxation falls outside the scope of the Convention","There is a violation, unless the State pays compensation equal to the lost rent","There is no violation, because the article does not restrict the State in enforcing laws necessary to control the use of property or to secure the payment of taxes"],3,null,"The third limb of P1-1 covers control of use and taxation. Montpellier is one of the French cities concerned.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Protocole 1 article 1 ; Plan IV","p11-003","concept"],["protocol-1-1","In Dokic v Bosnia and Herzegovina [2010], the Court found a violation of Protocol 1 article 1 because:",["The applicant''s property had been expropriated without any legal basis","The applicant was treated differently from others on the basis of his Serbian ethnicity when seeking to repossess his pre-war home","The applicant had been taxed on a property he no longer occupied","The applicant''s flat had been destroyed during the war without compensation"],1,null,"A lecturer at a military school who had vacated his Sarajevo flat during the 1992-1995 war and was refused repossession afterwards.",3,"The course handout attributes the violation to a difference in treatment based on the applicant''s Serbian ethnicity. The Court in fact found a disproportionate interference with the peaceful enjoyment of possessions (no repossession and no compensation) and did not examine article 14 separately.",null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Protocol 1 Article 1","p11-004","case"],["court","How many judges sit on the European Court of Human Rights?",["17, the members of the Grand Chamber","46, one per Member State of the Council of Europe","7, the members of a Chamber","27, one per Member State of the European Union"],1,null,"One judge per Member State of the Council of Europe. 17 and 7 are the sizes of the Grand Chamber and of a Chamber.",2,"The printed handout still says 47, before Russia ceased to be a member in 2022.",null,"CM Anglais, The European Court of Human Rights ; Plan I.B","crt-001","figure"],["court","A committee of the European Court of Human Rights is composed of:",["Seven judges","Three judges","A single judge","Seventeen judges"],1,null,"Single judge and three-judge committee for admissibility or for merits already covered by established case law; seven-judge Chamber for other cases; Grand Chamber of 17 for the most serious.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Composition of the Court ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 4.","crt-002","figure"],["court","A Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights is composed of:",["Seven judges","Seventeen judges","Three judges","Five judges"],0,null,"Seven-judge Chamber.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Composition of the Court ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 4.","crt-003","figure"],["court","The Grand Chamber of the European Court of Human Rights is composed of:",["Seventeen judges","Forty-six judges","Seven judges","Twenty-one judges"],0,null,"17 judges, for cases raising serious questions or undecided points of law.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Composition of the Court ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 4.","crt-004","figure"],["court","Which formation may rule on a case concerning an issue already covered by established case law?",["The plenary Court","A single judge or a three-judge committee","A seven-judge Chamber only","The Grand Chamber only"],1,null,"Established case law is handled by the lightest formations, which is what allows the Court to cope with its caseload.",3,"As taught. In strict terms a single judge may only declare an application inadmissible or strike it out; only a three-judge committee may rule on the merits of a matter covered by well-established case law (article 28).",null,"CM Anglais, Composition of the Court ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 4.","crt-005","concept"],["court","How is the Grand Chamber composed, according to the course handout?",["One judge from each Member State, drawn by lot","The Court''s President and Vice-Presidents, the Section Presidents and the national judge, excluding any judge who first examined the case","The seventeen most senior judges of the Court","The judges of the Chamber that delivered the judgment, plus ten others"],1,null,"The exclusion of the judges who first examined the case is the point most likely to be tested.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), 4.","crt-006","concept"],["court","According to the course, how are cases usually considered before the Court?",["Through an oral hearing in Strasbourg in every case","Through an oral hearing only where the applicant requests one","Through a hearing held in the respondent State","In writing, without any need for the applicant or the defending State to appear in Strasbourg, on the basis of the parties'' submissions and supporting documents"],3,null,"In exceptional cases going to the Grand Chamber, representatives of the applicant and of the State may attend a hearing. The principle of disclosure must be respected throughout.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, How do you take a case to court ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 3.","crt-007","concept"],["court","The European Court of Human Rights has the power to hold States accountable for a failure to respect, protect and fulfil Convention rights. In that respect, the Court has the power:",["To fine a State in order to sanction it for a violation","To find a State responsible and compel it to amend its Constitution","To find a State responsible for a violation of a Convention right and order it to amend its legislation to make it comply with the Convention","To annul national laws or overrule a judgment issued by a national court where these are in breach of the Convention"],2,null,"The Court has no power to annul domestic laws or quash domestic judgments; it condemns the State and requires it to redress the violation, including by general measures. Nor does it fine States: just satisfaction compensates the applicant, it does not punish the State. This is annales Question 14.",2,"As taught, and as required by annales Question 14. In strict terms the Court''s judgments are declaratory: the State chooses the general measures, under the supervision of the Committee of Ministers, except in pilot judgments where the Court indicates them.",null,"CM Anglais, The European Court of Human Rights ; QCM ANGLAIS Q14","crt-008","concept"],["court","The notion of ''general measures'' refers to:",["The request made by a Member State to be allowed temporarily not to apply some Convention rights due to specific circumstances","The general obligation for a Member State to protect and respect human rights","Any compensation paid by a Member State in breach of the Convention to the victim of that breach","A change in the domestic legislation of a Member State in order to make it comply with the Convention"],3,null,"General measures ensure the violation does not happen again (policy or legislative change); specific measures redress the individual loss (compensation). The wording about a State asking to be allowed temporarily not to apply certain rights describes a derogation under article 15; the wording about a general obligation to protect and respect human rights describes article 1. This is annales Question 4.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, When the court makes a judgement ; Plan VI.G ; QCM ANGLAIS Q4","crt-009","concept"],["court","The notion of ''specific measures'' refers to:",["The measures taken by the Committee of Ministers to enforce a judgment","The measures taken by a State to remedy the loss caused to the individual applicant, typically compensation","The amendment of domestic legislation so that the violation does not recur","Interim measures ordered by the Court before it rules on the merits"],1,null,"Specific for the individual, general for the system. A recurring pair in QCMs.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, When the court makes a judgement ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 5.","crt-010","concept"],["court","Which body enforces the judgments of the European Court of Human Rights?",["The Grand Chamber of the Court","The Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe","The Committee of Ministers of the Council of Europe, composed of the Ministers of Foreign Affairs of the Member States or their permanent diplomatic representatives","The Registry of the Court"],2,null,"The Committee of Ministers is the Council of Europe''s decision-making body and monitors the implementation of judgments, both specific and general measures.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), 6.","crt-011","concept"],["court","Which of the following changes was NOT presented in the course as a step taken by a State following a judgment of the European Court?",["The United Kingdom banned corporal punishment in schools","France abolished the death penalty","Ireland introduced legal aid in civil cases","Bulgaria created an alternative to military service for conscientious objectors"],1,null,"The examples given are: Airey (civil legal aid in Ireland), Norris (decriminalisation of homosexuality in Ireland), Johnston (equal status for children born inside or outside marriage), Marckx v Belgium, K.A. v Finland (children in foster care), Campbell and Cosans v UK (corporal punishment), Burdov v Russia (Chernobyl victims), Stefanov v Bulgaria (conscientious objectors).",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), 5. Examples","crt-012","case"],["court","Following which judgment did Ireland decriminalise homosexuality?",["Modinos v Cyprus [1994]","Norris v Ireland [1988]","Dudgeon v UK [1982]","Johnston v Ireland [1986]"],1,null,"The sequence is Dudgeon for the UK, then Norris for Ireland, then Modinos for Cyprus.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), 5. Examples and Article 8","crt-013","case"],["court","Marckx v Belgium [1979] led Belgium to:",["Create an alternative to compulsory military service","Introduce legal aid in civil cases","Take steps to stop discrimination against children born outside marriage","Abolish corporal punishment in schools"],2,null,"Part of the same family of cases as Johnston v Ireland and the French adultery-succession case of 2000.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), 5. Examples","crt-014","case"],["principles","The proportionality test applied by the Court requires that any restriction satisfies three conditions. Which of the following is NOT one of them?",["The restriction must have a legitimate aim","The restriction must limit the right to the least degree possible","The restriction must be clearly connected to achieving that aim","The restriction must be compensated financially"],3,null,"Legitimate aim, rational connection, minimal impairment. The lecturer''s image: you cannot use a sledgehammer to crack a nut.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of proportionality ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V.A","prn-001","concept"],["principles","In MS v Sweden [1997], the Court found no violation of article 8 because:",["The transfer of confidential medical information from one public authority to another, without the patient''s consent, was not out of proportion to the legitimate aim of checking the information received from the patient against the clinic''s data","Sweden enjoyed a wide margin of appreciation in health matters","The patient had implicitly consented to the transfer by submitting a claim","Medical data does not fall within the scope of private life"],0,null,"The course''s leading illustration of the proportionality test, and note the outcome: NO violation.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of proportionality ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V.A","prn-002","case"],["principles","The principle of ''positive obligations'' means that:",["States must incorporate the Convention into their domestic legal order","States must ratify every additional protocol","States must ensure Convention rights are protected in a proactive way, rather than merely refraining from violating them","States must compensate every applicant who suffers a violation"],2,null,"Kontrová v Slovakia (article 2), Siliadin v France (article 4) and Öneryildiz v Turkey (article 2) are the course''s illustrations.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Positive obligations ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V.B","prn-003","concept"],["principles","In Öneryildiz v Turkey [2004], the Court found a violation of article 2 because:",["The State was under a positive obligation to put in place an administrative and legislative framework protecting against threats to life, and no one had been charged after a methane explosion at a municipal rubbish dump killed several residents","Turkey had allowed residents to settle near a dangerous site despite their own awareness of the risk","Turkey had failed to investigate a suspicious death in police custody","Turkish police officers had used lethal force against demonstrators"],0,null,"The case shows that negligence may engage positive obligations: the State must put in place an administrative and legislative framework protecting life and ensure that any threat to it is properly punished. The State''s argument that the victims had chosen to live beside the dump was rejected. Note that no one was ultimately held criminally accountable for the loss of life, which the Court treated as part of the violation.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Positive Obligations","prn-004","case"],["principles","The ''margin of appreciation'' is:",["The time limit within which a State must execute a judgment","The discretion of the Court to decide which cases it will examine","The percentage of just satisfaction a State may withhold","The degree of discretion the Court allows Member States when interpreting the Convention and applying its rights in their own countries"],3,null,"It reflects the diversity of cultural and legal traditions among the Member States, but it never permits a violation of rights.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary and Section 5 ; Plan V.C","prn-005","vocab"],["principles","In which field is the margin of appreciation left to States described as being WIDE?",["Family life","Discrimination","Taxation","Prohibition of torture"],2,null,"Wide on matters of taxation, to allow for different fiscal and administrative structures; much narrower on family life and discrimination. There is no margin at all in respect of absolute rights.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Margin of appreciation ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V.C","prn-006","concept"],["principles","In which fields is the margin of appreciation described as being NARROW?",["National security and public safety","Family life and discrimination","Taxation and fiscal policy","Education and religion"],1,null,"Careful with education and religion: Lautsi shows that a wide margin applies there where no European consensus exists.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Margin of appreciation ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V.C","prn-007","concept"],["principles","In Lautsi v Italy [2011], concerning crucifixes displayed on classroom walls in State schools, what was the final outcome?",["The Court found a violation of Protocol 1 article 2 taken together with article 9, and Italy removed the crucifixes","The application was declared inadmissible","The Court found a violation of article 9 alone","The Grand Chamber overturned the initial finding of a violation, holding that States enjoy a wide margin of appreciation on social or moral issues such as religion and education where there is no European consensus"],3,null,"The two-stage history matters: a violation at first, then reversal by the Grand Chamber. The initial finding was based on P1-2 (right to education) together with article 9.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Margin of appreciation ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V.C","prn-008","case"],["principles","Which of the following is NOT one of the guiding principles of the Court set out in the course?",["Reciprocity between States parties","Margin of appreciation","Positive obligations","Proportionality"],0,null,"The handout''s list is proportionality, positive obligations, margin of appreciation, and equality and non-discrimination.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5 ; Plan V","prn-009","concept"],["principles","To which category of rights does the proportionality principle apply?",["Only the rights contained in the additional protocols","All Convention rights without distinction","Absolute rights only","Qualified rights only"],3,null,"Where a right is absolute, no interference whatsoever is allowed, so there is nothing to weigh.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of proportionality ; Plan V.A","prn-010","concept"],["subsidiarity","In the context of the European Convention on Human Rights, the principle of subsidiarity means that:",["It is only if national courts declared the claim inadmissible that the claim may be reviewed by the Court","It is only if national courts have had the opportunity to consider and redress the alleged violation and failed to provide redress that an application may be made to the European Court of Human Rights","It is only if national courts dismissed the claim on its merits that the claim may be reviewed by the Court","It is only in respect of qualified rights that alleged violations must be brought before national courts first, alleged violations of absolute rights being submitted directly to the Court"],1,null,"Subsidiarity makes no distinction between absolute and qualified rights, and it is not conditional on the domestic outcome taking a particular form: what matters is that the national courts had the opportunity to redress the violation and did not. This is annales Question 1.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A ; QCM ANGLAIS Q1","sub-001","concept"],["subsidiarity","Before which national courts must a Convention violation be pleaded?",["At any time during the proceedings, including before a national Supreme Court for the first time","Before all the national courts, including the courts of first instance","Only from the appeal stage of the proceedings","Only before the national Supreme Court"],1,null,"The violation must be raised from the outset, at first instance, and pleaded consistently through appeal and before the highest court. This is annales Question 13.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A ; QCM ANGLAIS Q13","sub-002","concept"],["subsidiarity","What does it mean to plead a violation ''substantively''?",["To state with specific reference to the applicable Convention articles why there is a violation, rather than merely alleging one in the abstract","To plead the violation before the highest domestic court","To support the violation with expert evidence","To plead the violation in writing rather than orally"],0,null,"The lecturer''s example: it is not enough to claim a violation of article 13 by the French authorities, you must say why.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A","sub-003","concept"],["subsidiarity","Why does the course insist that lawyers should always seek to have the violation established by the national courts?",["Because the time limit only starts running once a national court has found a violation","Because the European Court is bound by the findings of the national courts","Because if a national court finds a violation, a subsequent application to the European Court becomes unnecessary","Because the European Court will only examine violations already found by a national court"],2,null,"The whole architecture rests on national courts being the first line of enforcement.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A","sub-004","concept"],["subsidiarity","Is it mandatory to appeal to the highest domestic court before applying to the European Court of Human Rights?",["No, the highest court need only be seised where an absolute right is at stake","No, an application may be lodged as soon as a first-instance court has dismissed the claim","In principle yes, otherwise the application risks being declared inadmissible under article 35, but there are two exceptions","Yes, without any exception"],2,null,"The two exceptions: where the highest court has already ruled on the point of law at issue (established case law), and where an appeal to the supreme court requires permission (leave to appeal), as in the United Kingdom.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A","sub-005","concept"],["subsidiarity","Which provision of the Convention lays down the requirement that all domestic remedies must have been exhausted?",["Article 35","Article 13","Article 41","Article 34"],0,null,"Article 35 governs admissibility. Article 41 is just satisfaction. Do not confuse article 13 (effective remedy at national level) with article 35 (exhaustion of remedies as a condition of admissibility).",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects of proceedings","sub-006","concept"],["subsidiarity","In a common law country where an appeal to the Supreme Court requires leave to appeal, an applicant who is refused leave:",["Is considered to have exhausted all domestic remedies","Is considered not to have exhausted domestic remedies, so the application is inadmissible","Must apply again for leave before turning to the European Court","Must ask the European Court for a waiver of the exhaustion requirement"],0,null,"A remedy that is not available as of right cannot be held against the applicant. In France, by contrast, the appeal to the Cour de cassation or Conseil d''État is as of right, so it must be used.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A","sub-007","concept"],["subsidiarity","How should the case law of the European Court be relied on before national courts?",["National courts are not permitted to take the Court''s case law into account","By referring to precedents concerning the same Convention article, including judgments delivered against other States, not only against the applicant''s own State","By referring only to Grand Chamber judgments","By referring exclusively to judgments delivered against the applicant''s own State, since only those are binding on it"],1,null,"The course''s key illustration is the Turkish judgment on the lawyer in police custody, which led France to change its own legislation. It is especially useful to consider decisions concerning countries with a similar legal system.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI.A","sub-008","concept"],["admissibility","Which of the following would make an application before the European Court of Human Rights inadmissible?",["The applicant was not directly the victim but only the victim''s legal representative","The applicant has not exhausted all the domestic remedies available","There is already established case law on the alleged violation, making a further ruling unnecessary","The applicant is not a national of the defendant State"],1,null,"Established case law does not make an application inadmissible: it allows a lighter formation (single judge or committee) to decide it. Nationality is irrelevant, since the Convention applies to everyone within the State''s jurisdiction. An official representative may act on behalf of a clearly identified victim. This is annales Question 3.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 2. ; QCM ANGLAIS Q3","adm-001","concept"],["admissibility","An application lodged by someone who was not the victim of the alleged violation:",["Would be admissible where the article allegedly violated protects an absolute right","Would be inadmissible","Would be referred to the relevant national court","Would be dismissed on the merits"],1,null,"The applicant must be directly and personally the victim. Note the difference between inadmissibility (the Court does not examine the merits at all) and dismissal on the merits. This is annales Question 19.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; QCM ANGLAIS Q19","adm-002","concept"],["admissibility","According to the lecture notes, which exception is mentioned to the rule that the applicant must be directly and personally the victim?",["Where the applicant is a minor","Where the violation concerns an absolute right","Where the applicant has died during the proceedings","Where the applicant is a non-governmental organisation"],0,null,"The lecture notes state it expressly: ''the claimant must be directly and personally the victim of the alleged violation. But, for one exception: when the claimant is a minor.'' The handout adds that a complaint may not be made on behalf of another person unless that person is clearly identified as their official representative.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 2. ; Plan VI.B","adm-003","concept"],["admissibility","Regarding anonymity before the Court:",["Applications may be lodged anonymously in all cases","Anonymity is available only where an absolute right is at stake","No application may be anonymous, but the applicant may ask the Court to preserve their anonymity, including vis-à-vis the respondent State","No application may be anonymous and the Court can never conceal the applicant''s identity"],2,null,"Two distinct things: identification of the applicant towards the Court (mandatory) and publicity of that identity (which may be restricted). This explains case names reduced to initials, such as Z v UK, DG v Ireland or MS v Sweden.",2,"The lecture notes go further and say the respondent State will not know who the applicant is. Under Rule 47 the applicant may ask that their identity not be disclosed TO THE PUBLIC; the respondent State necessarily knows it. Answer as taught, but do not write this in an essay.",null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; Plan VI.B","adm-004","concept"],["admissibility","Which of the following is NOT a condition of admissibility listed in the course?",["All domestic remedies must have been exhausted within the time limit","The case must not have been brought before the European Court of Human Rights previously","The complaint may only concern rights contained in the Convention","The applicant must be represented by a lawyer when lodging the application"],3,null,"The course does not make legal representation a condition of admissibility; where the applicant does have a lawyer, the authority given to that lawyer must be part of the application form, completed, dated and signed.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 2. ; Plan VI.B","adm-005","concept"],["admissibility","What remedies are available against a decision of the European Court declaring an application inadmissible?",["Regularisation of the application within a week of the decision","None: a decision declaring an application inadmissible is final and cannot be challenged","A new application lodged within a week of the decision","An appeal to the Appeal Chamber of the Court"],1,null,"There is no Appeal Chamber at the Court. Inadmissibility decisions, and judgments of committees and of the Grand Chamber, cannot be appealed. This is annales Question 15.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The enforcement ; Plan VI.G ; QCM ANGLAIS Q15","adm-006","concept"],["admissibility","Can a State bring a case against another State before the European Court of Human Rights?",["Yes, but only where the two States have made a declaration accepting the Court''s jurisdiction for such cases","No, only individuals may lodge applications","Yes, but only with the prior authorisation of the Committee of Ministers","Yes, inter-State cases are possible though rare, and usually concern important matters"],3,null,"Ireland v UK [1978] was the first such case.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 2.","adm-007","concept"],["admissibility","What is the first thing the Court deals with when it receives an application?",["Whether interim measures should be granted","The merits of the alleged violation","The amount of just satisfaction claimed","The admissibility of the case"],3,null,"If the case is inadmissible, the Court will not decide it on the merits, however well founded the complaint might be.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, How do you take a case to court","adm-008","concept"],["time-limit","According to the course, within what period must an application be lodged with the Court?",["One year from the final domestic decision","Three months from the final domestic decision","Six months from the final domestic decision, or from the date it was notified to the applicant or their lawyer","Four months from the final domestic decision"],2,null,"The course teaches six months. IMPORTANT: since Protocol No. 15 entered into force, the time limit has in fact been four months for final domestic decisions taken on or after 1 February 2022. Answer six months in this exam, but know the real rule.",2,"Positive law: the time limit is now FOUR months (Protocol No. 15, applicable since 1 February 2022). The course has not been updated.",null,"CM Anglais, The time limit ; Plan VI.C","tim-001","figure"],["time-limit","When the deadline for an application before the European Court falls on a Saturday, a Sunday or a public holiday:",["The deadline is extended by 12 hours on the next working day","The deadline is not extended","The deadline is extended to the next whole working day only where an absolute right is at stake","The deadline is extended to the next whole working day"],1,null,"The period ends on the last day even if that day is a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday, unlike French procedural law. This is annales Question 20.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, The time limit ; Plan VI.C ; QCM ANGLAIS Q20","tim-002","concept"],["time-limit","From what point does the time limit for lodging an application run?",["From the date of the final decision of the highest competent national authority, or from the date that decision was notified to the applicant or their lawyer","From the date the first-instance court delivered its decision","From the date the applicant instructed a lawyer","From the date the violation occurred"],0,null,"The starting point is the final domestic decision closing the exhaustion of remedies, or its notification.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, The time limit ; Plan VI.C","tim-003","concept"],["time-limit","What interrupts the running of the time limit?",["Only the lodging of a complete application, with all the relevant documents","Sending the application by fax","Sending any letter to the Registry indicating an intention to apply","Sending the application by e-mail"],0,null,"Fax and e-mail do not count as lodging an application. This is a classic QCM trap.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The time limit ; Plan VI.C","tim-004","concept"],["time-limit","For the purposes of the time limit, what counts?",["The receipt of the complete application by the Registry, not the date of sending","The date of posting, as evidenced by the postmark","The date the applicant signed the application form","The date the applicant''s lawyer was instructed"],0,null,"Hence the practical advice: post well in advance by recorded delivery, deliver the application in person, or have it delivered by a bailiff.",3,"As taught. Under Rule 47 §6(a) of the Rules of Court as currently in force, the relevant date is the date of DISPATCH (the postmark), not the date of receipt. The course has not been updated on this point.",null,"CM Anglais, The time limit ; Plan VI.C","tim-005","concept"],["time-limit","An application is incomplete and is therefore not taken into account by the Court. What can the applicant do?",["Nothing: the application is definitively lost","Appeal against the Registry''s refusal to register the application","Submit a new application, provided it is still submitted within the time limit","Ask the Registry to regularise the existing application within a week"],2,null,"The new application enjoys no extension of the time limit, which is why an incomplete application filed at the last minute is fatal.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Notion of sending an application ; Plan VI.D","tim-006","concept"],["filing","How must an application be lodged with the Court?",["Through the national ministry of justice, which forwards it to Strasbourg","By any written means addressed to the Registry, the form being optional","By using the Court''s official application form, available on the Court''s website, sent by post to the Registry","By e-mail to the Registry, with the original to follow by post"],2,null,"Failure to provide precise and accurate information on the form may result in the application not being considered.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Notion of sending an application ; Plan VI.D","fil-001","concept"],["filing","Which of the following documents is NOT listed in the course among those that should be attached to an application?",["Documents confirming compliance with the time limit, such as the notification of the highest court''s decision","Copies of the decisions of the national courts","A certificate of nationality of the applicant","Copies of the submissions relied on before the national courts showing that the Convention was raised there"],2,null,"Nationality is irrelevant to the Convention. Other documents may be attached, such as transcripts, medical or other reports, and witness statements.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, documents ; Plan VI.D","fil-002","concept"],["filing","Why does the course insist on attaching the submissions made before the national courts?",["Because they establish the applicant''s nationality and residence","Because the Court needs them in order to calculate just satisfaction","Because judgments do not always address the Convention points of law raised by the lawyers, so the submissions are the proof that the violation was pleaded domestically","Because the Court will not examine any argument that was not made domestically in writing"],2,null,"This is the evidential link between subsidiarity and admissibility.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, documents ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","fil-003","concept"],["filing","Where the applicant is represented, the authority given to the lawyer:",["Must be drawn up by a notary in the applicant''s State","Must be part of the application form and must be completed, dated and signed by the applicant","Must be sent separately to the Registry within a month of the application","Is not required, since the Court presumes the lawyer''s authority"],1,null,"A formal point that lends itself well to QCM testing.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","fil-004","concept"],["interim-measures","Interim measures before the European Court are:",["Temporary measures ordered before the case is decided on the merits, where waiting for the final decision would make the harm irreparable","Measures adopted by the Committee of Ministers pending execution of a judgment","Measures ordered by the Court to enforce its final judgment","Measures taken by the respondent State to redress an individual violation"],0,null,"The course''s example: preventing an extradition to a country where the applicant faces the death penalty, before the Court has ruled.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Interim measures ; Plan VI.E","int-001","concept"],["interim-measures","Interim measures ordered by the Court are:",["Available only where an absolute right is at stake","Merely recommendations that the State is free to follow or not","Mandatory for the State concerned, but granted only in exceptional cases, mainly concerning extradition and deportation","Mandatory for the State and granted in every case where they are requested"],2,null,"Binding, but exceptional, and concentrated in removal cases.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Interim measures ; Plan VI.E","int-002","concept"],["interim-measures","Where a request for interim measures is dismissed:",["The substantive application is automatically withdrawn","The applicant may appeal to the Grand Chamber","The applicant may lodge a fresh request within a week","No appeal is allowed, and the applicant must inform the Court whether the substantive application will be continued"],3,null,"Two cumulative consequences: no remedy against the refusal, and a duty to tell the Court whether the case goes on.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Interim measures ; Plan VI.E","int-003","concept"],["interim-measures","A request for interim measures must:",["Be filed before the domestic remedies have been exhausted","Be supported by a declaration from the applicant''s State of origin","Be justified and explain in detail the grounds of the applicant''s fears, the nature of the risks alleged and the Convention provisions said to be violated, with the domestic decisions attached","Simply identify the measure sought and the date of the planned removal"],2,null,"Detailed justification plus the domestic decisions of the courts and other domestic bodies.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Interim measures ; Plan VI.E","int-004","concept"],["just-satisfaction","Under which article of the Convention does the Court have the power to award just satisfaction?",["Article 13","Article 34","Article 41","Article 35"],2,null,"Article 41. Article 35 concerns admissibility and exhaustion of remedies.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","jst-001","article"],["just-satisfaction","Just satisfaction may compensate:",["Legal costs and expenses only","Financial damage only","Non-financial damage only, financial loss being a matter for the national courts","Financial and non-financial damage, such as mental or physical suffering, as well as legal costs and expenses"],3,null,"It is equivalent to damages for the injured party.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","jst-002","concept"],["just-satisfaction","What is the function of just satisfaction?",["To punish the State in breach and deter future violations","Both to compensate the applicant and to fine the State","To compensate applicants, not to punish Member States","To fund the execution of general measures by the State"],2,null,"This compensatory, non-punitive character explains why an application may be declared inadmissible where the applicant has suffered no significant damage. It also disposes of the ''fine the State'' distractor in annales Question 14.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","jst-003","concept"],["just-satisfaction","How must a claim for just satisfaction be presented?",["Orally at the hearing before the Chamber","Through the applicant''s national authorities","As a single global figure, which the Court will then adjust","Itemised and supported by relevant documents, failing which no award will be made"],3,null,"No itemisation and no supporting documents means no award, however real the violation.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","jst-004","concept"],["just-satisfaction","Can a legal person be awarded just satisfaction?",["Yes, but only for financial loss, never for non-financial damage","No, only natural persons may be compensated","Yes, for instance for damage to the company''s image or uncertainty in planning decisions","Only where the legal person is a non-governmental organisation"],2,null,"The Court can award compensation for non-financial loss to a legal person too.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","jst-005","concept"],["just-satisfaction","An application for just satisfaction may be declared inadmissible where:",["The applicant has already lodged a request for interim measures","The applicant is a legal person rather than a natural person","The violation concerns a qualified rather than an absolute right","The Court considers that the applicant has suffered no significant damage"],3,null,"A direct consequence of the compensatory function. Note that a legal person may perfectly well be awarded just satisfaction, for damage to its image for instance.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","jst-006","concept"],["enforcement","Which decisions of the Court cannot be appealed?",["All decisions of the Court without exception, including Chamber judgments","Admissibility decisions and judgments delivered by committees and by the Grand Chamber","Only admissibility decisions","Only judgments of the Grand Chamber"],1,null,"A Chamber judgment is the exception: a party may request that the case be referred to the Grand Chamber for review, though such review is exceptional.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The enforcement ; Plan VI.G","enf-001","concept"],["enforcement","A party is dissatisfied with a judgment delivered by a Chamber. What can it do?",["Nothing: Chamber judgments are final in all cases","Appeal to the Committee of Ministers","Lodge a new application on the same facts","Request that the case be referred to the Grand Chamber for review, which is granted exceptionally"],3,null,"It is a referral request, not an appeal as of right.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The enforcement ; Plan VI.G","enf-002","concept"],["enforcement","When the Court finds a violation, the respondent State:",["Must carry out all the obligations resulting from the judgment and take the steps required to redress the violation, through specific and general measures","May choose whether or not to execute the judgment, since the Court has no enforcement power","Must automatically amend its Constitution","Must submit the judgment to its parliament for ratification"],0,null,"Judgments are binding on the States concerned; the Committee of Ministers monitors implementation.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, When the court makes a judgement ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 6.","enf-003","concept"],["vocabulary","''Admissible'' means:",["Lodged within the applicable time limit","Well founded on the merits","Allowed to be heard in court: the case has passed certain technical requirements and will be considered by the court","Accepted by the respondent State"],2,null,"Admissibility is a threshold question, entirely separate from whether the complaint is well founded.",1,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-001","vocab"],["vocabulary","The ''applicant'' is:",["The lawyer representing a party before the Court","The person who is bringing a case before a court","The State against which a case is brought","The judge assigned to examine the case"],1,null,"Note the French false friend: ''applicant'' is the requérant, not the ''appliquant''.",1,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-002","vocab"],["vocabulary","''Binding'' signifies:",["A provisional measure","A procedural formality","An obligation, or legal duty","A recommendation with no legal force"],2,null,"Judgments are binding on the States concerned; interim measures are also mandatory for the State.",1,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-003","vocab"],["vocabulary","''To contravene'' a right means:",["To go against or deny a right contained in the Convention","To derogate from a right in time of emergency","To invoke a right before a national court","To restrict a right in a proportionate manner"],0,null,"A synonym of ''to breach'' or ''to violate''.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-004","vocab"],["vocabulary","A ''domestic remedy'' is:",["The compensation awarded by the European Court","The solutions available to an applicant at national level through national courts, courts of appeal or tribunals","A remedy provided for by the Convention itself","A remedy available only before the highest national court"],1,null,"Hence the requirement to ''exhaust domestic remedies'' before applying to Strasbourg.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-005","vocab"],["vocabulary","''Foreseeability'' is:",["The ability of the Court to anticipate the consequences of its own judgments","The requirement that the law or administrative measure be sufficiently clear and predictable to allow a person reasonably to foresee the outcome of their actions","The requirement that a judgment be executed within a predictable time","The obligation on States to plan for public emergencies"],1,null,"Closely connected to the principle of legal certainty under article 7.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-006","vocab"],["vocabulary","When a convention or treaty is ''in force'', this means:",["The instrument has been incorporated into domestic law","The instrument has been adopted by the Committee of Ministers","A State has agreed to be bound by the instrument and its provisions now apply","The instrument has been signed but not yet ratified"],2,null,"The ECHR was written in 1950 and came into force in 1953.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-007","vocab"],["vocabulary","Rights are said to be ''inherent'', which means:",["They are enforceable only before an international court","They are entitlements which belong to everyone by virtue of being human","They are granted to individuals by their State","They may never be restricted"],1,null,"Inherence is why the State does not ''give'' rights: it must respect, protect and fulfil them.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-008","vocab"],["vocabulary","''Jurisdiction'' is defined in the course as:",["The competence of a national court to hear a given dispute","The territory of a State, strictly defined by its borders","The power of the European Court to examine an application","The extent or territory over which legal or other power extends; it may reach beyond a State''s territorial boundaries and includes any activity carried out under the control of the State"],3,null,"The extraterritorial element is what made Hirsi Jamaa v Italy possible.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-009","vocab"],["vocabulary","''Leave to appeal'' means:",["The time limit within which an appeal must be lodged","The permission that must be obtained from a court, typically a supreme court, before an appeal may be brought","The withdrawal of an appeal by the appellant","The right to appeal available automatically to any losing party"],1,null,"Where leave is refused, the applicant is nonetheless treated as having exhausted domestic remedies. In France the appeal to the Cour de cassation or Conseil d''État is as of right, so no leave is needed.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, exhaustion of remedies ; Plan VI.A, vocabulary list","voc-010","vocab"],["vocabulary","''To ratify'' means:",["That a State enters a reservation to part of a treaty","That a State incorporates a treaty into its domestic legislation","That a State signifies its agreement to be legally bound by the terms of a particular convention or treaty","That a State signs a treaty at a diplomatic conference"],2,null,"Ratification is what makes a protocol binding on a given State: signature alone is not enough.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-011","vocab"],["vocabulary","A ''state party'' to a convention is:",["A country that has signed but not ratified the treaty","A country against which an application has been lodged","Any Member State of the Council of Europe","A country that has agreed to be bound by that treaty and is legally bound by its provisions"],3,null,"Careful not to confuse ''State party'' with ''party to proceedings''.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-012","vocab"],["vocabulary","A ''provision'' is:",["The content of a particular article within a law, treaty or convention","A temporary measure ordered by the Court","A condition of admissibility","A sum set aside by a State to pay just satisfaction"],0,null,"Another false friend to keep in mind alongside ''provisional measures''.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-013","vocab"],["vocabulary","In the course, ''remedy'' primarily means:",["The compensation granted by a court","The possibility of challenging a decision, which under article 13 must also be effective","The execution of a judgment by the respondent State","The correction of a procedural irregularity"],1,null,"The lecturer distinguishes the two possible senses and adopts the first: ''remedy'' as the possibility of challenging a decision, rather than as what the judge grants.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIII ; Plan VI, vocabulary list","voc-014","vocab"],["vocabulary","''To uphold a decision'' means:",["That the court of appeal sets aside the decision made by the first court","That the court stays the execution of the decision","That the court of appeal confirms the decision made by the first court","That the court refers the case back to the first court"],2,null,"The opposite is ''to reverse a decision''. Note also ''to provide relief'', meaning to grant the applicant''s claim.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Vocab","voc-015","vocab"],["vocabulary","''To lodge'' or ''to file'' an application means:",["To notify the application to the respondent State","To submit the application to the Court''s Registry","To register the application in a national court","To publish the application on the Court''s website"],1,null,"And only a complete application, received by the Registry, counts as lodged.",3,null,null,"Plan VI, vocabulary list ; CM Anglais, The time limit","voc-016","vocab"],["vocabulary","''To redress'' a violation means:",["To acknowledge publicly that a violation has occurred","To request the referral of a case to the Grand Chamber","To declare an application admissible","To put right the violation, through specific and general measures"],3,null,"Subsidiarity is expressed precisely in these terms: the national courts must have had the opportunity to consider and redress the alleged violation.",3,null,null,"Plan VI, vocabulary list ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","voc-017","vocab"],["vocabulary","In the course''s terms, ''Convention'', ''covenant'', ''treaty'' and ''agreement'':",["Are distinguished by the number of States that have ratified them","Designate four distinct categories of instrument with different legal force","Designate instruments of the Council of Europe, the United Nations, the European Union and the OSCE respectively","Are often used interchangeably to designate a formal international agreement between States"],3,null,"Glossary definition.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-018","vocab"],["vocabulary","In the Convention''s terminology, the phrase ''rights and freedoms'':",["Distinguishes the rights in the Convention from those in the protocols","Distinguishes civil and political rights from economic and social rights","Is simply another way of talking about rights","Distinguishes absolute rights from qualified rights"],2,null,"Glossary definition: ''freedoms'' is another way of talking about rights.",2,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","voc-019","vocab"],["vocabulary","Which pair correctly matches the English term with its French equivalent as given in the course?",["Domestic remedy = satisfaction équitable","Interim measures = mesures générales","Interim measures = mesures conservatoires","Just satisfaction = mesures conservatoires"],2,null,"The course also glosses ''pleadings and submissions'' as conclusions or mémoires.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","voc-020","vocab"],["annales","In the context of the European Convention of Human Rights, the principle of subsidiarity means that:",["It is only in respect of qualified rights that alleged violation(s) of the Convention must be brought before national courts first whereas alleged violation(s) of absolute rights can be submitted directly to the European Court of Human Rights","It is only if national courts have had the opportunity to consider and redress the alleged violation(s) of the Convention and failed to provide redress that an application may be made to the European Court of Human Rights","It is only if national courts dismissed a claim on its merits regarding the alleged violation(s) of the Convention that the claim at issue can be reviewed by the European Court of Human Rights","It is only if national courts declared a claim inadmissible regarding the alleged violation(s) of the Convention that the claim at issue can be reviewed by the European Court of Human Rights"],1,null,"The course''s own formulation: ''The principle of subsidiarity requires that national courts must always have the opportunity to consider and to redress the alleged violation of the Convention. It is only if they have not provided redress that an application to the ECHR is possible.'' Subsidiarity draws no distinction between absolute and qualified rights, and does not depend on the form the domestic outcome took.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 1 ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","ann-01","exam"],["annales","Which of the following statements is correct?",["Domestic courts are bound by the decisions of the European Court of Human Rights against their national State and must take into account the decisions made against other States that have a similar legal system","Domestic courts are bound by the decisions of the European Court of Human Rights against other States but are totally free to ignore the decisions made against other States","Domestic courts are not bound by the decisions of the European Court of Human Rights but must take them into account","Domestic courts are not bound by the decisions of the European Court of Human Rights and may disregard them entirely"],0,null,"The course states: ''When a decision is made by a state, only this state is binding by the decision of the ECHR, but this decision should still be the same for the other states'', and advises considering decisions concerning ''countries with a similar legal system''. The correct answer reproduces that two-stage structure almost word for word: bound as regards judgments against one''s own State, bound to take into account those against States with a similar legal system. The answer saying domestic courts are bound by decisions against other States but free to ignore decisions against other States contradicts itself.",3,null,"The answer stating that domestic courts are not bound but must take the case law into account is the standard UK Human Rights Act formulation, and is defensible if your lecturer taught that ECtHR judgments are declaratory and never bind domestic courts as such. The wording of the course notes points instead to the two-stage answer retained here. Note also that the sujet contains an apparent typo: one distractor says domestic courts are bound by decisions against other States but free to ignore decisions against other States, which is self-contradictory as printed. Worth clarifying in class.","QCM ANGLAIS, Question 2 ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","ann-02","exam"],["annales","Which of the following would make an application before the European Court of Human Rights inadmissible:",["The applicant has not exhausted all the domestic remedies available","There is already established case law by the European Court of Human Rights regarding the alleged violation, hence making it unnecessary for the Court to issue another decision","The applicant was not directly the victim of the violation but only the victim''s legal representative","The applicant is not a national of the defendant State"],0,null,"Established case law does not bar an application: it allows a single judge or a three-judge committee to rule on it. An official legal representative of a clearly identified victim may act. Nationality is irrelevant, since the Convention protects everyone within the State''s jurisdiction.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 3 ; CM Anglais, conditions","ann-03","exam"],["annales","The notion of ''general measures'' can refer to:",["The general obligation for a Member State to protect and respect human rights","Any compensation paid by a Member State that was in breach of the Convention to the victim of the breach","A change in the domestic legislation of a Member State in order to make it comply with the Convention","The request made by a Member State to the Court to be allowed temporarily not to apply all or some of the rights set out in the Convention due to specific circumstances"],2,null,"General measures prevent recurrence (legislative or policy change); specific measures remedy the individual loss, which is what the answer about compensation paid to the victim describes. The answer about a temporary request not to apply certain rights describes a derogation under article 15; the answer about a general obligation to protect and respect human rights describes article 1.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 4 ; CM Anglais, When the court makes a judgement","ann-04","exam"],["annales","The fact that the rights set out in the Convention are ''interdependent and indivisible'' means that:",["All the rights set out in the Convention are intertwined and the fact of not respecting one of them may adversely affect another","There must be a violation of at least two rights protected by the Convention for an application to be lodged with the Court","All the rights set out in the Convention are of equal value","Everyone is entitled to these rights in equal measure, without discrimination"],0,null,"Glossary definition from the course: ''Rights are linked to each other in such a way that non-observance of one right may impact on another.'' The answer about everyone being entitled to these rights in equal measure without discrimination describes universality and non-discrimination, a different idea. Note the risk: ''all rights are of equal value'' is the standard gloss of ''indivisible'' in Council of Europe glossaries, so it is the most dangerous distractor here. The course''s own definition is decisive.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 5 ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary","ann-05","exam"],["annales","An ''absolute right'':",["Is a right that can be enforced exclusively by the European Court of Human Rights","Is a right belonging only to the nationals of the Member States that did not opt out of articles 3, 4 and 7","Is a right which can be interfered with or derogated from under limited circumstances to protect specific public interests","Is a right regarding which no derogation is allowed"],3,null,"The answer describing a right that can be interfered with under limited circumstances to protect specific public interests is the definition of a QUALIFIED right, which is the trap.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 6 ; CM Anglais, The nature of rights","ann-06","exam"],["annales","The right to Respect for Private and Family Life:",["May not be interfered with by a public authority under any circumstances, except in the interest of the national security, public safety or prevention of crime or to protect other people''s rights and freedoms, even if the interference is not proportionate","Must not be interfered with by a public authority under any circumstances","Must be interfered with by a public authority if it is in accordance with the law, in the interest of the national security, public safety or prevention of crime or to protect other people''s rights and freedoms, even if the interference is not proportionate","May be interfered with by a public authority if it is in accordance with the law, in the interest of the national security, public safety or prevention of crime or to protect other people''s rights and freedoms and if the interference is proportionate"],3,null,"Article 8 is a qualified right: legality, legitimate aim and proportionality are cumulative. Every other option either drops proportionality or turns a faculty (''may'') into an obligation (''must'').",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 7 ; CM Anglais, Article VIII","ann-07","exam"],["annales","Regarding the protection of the rights guaranteed by the European Convention on Human Rights, the notion of ''proportionality'' refers to:",["The possibility for a State to interfere with one or several rights protected by the Convention, whether qualified or absolute, provided that the interference is necessary and proportionate to a legitimate aim to be achieved","The fact that it is not possible for a State to interfere with one or several absolute rights protected by the Convention since the rights protected by the Convention are inherent to everyone","The possibility for a State to interfere with one or several qualified rights protected by the Convention provided that the interference is necessary and proportionate to a legitimate aim to be achieved","The possibility for a State to interfere with one or several absolute rights protected by the Convention provided that the interference is necessary and proportionate to a legitimate aim to be achieved"],2,null,"Proportionality concerns qualified rights only. The answer covering rights ''whether qualified or absolute'' is tempting but wrong precisely because it extends the test to absolute rights, where no interference whatsoever is permitted.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 8 ; CM Anglais, Principle of proportionality","ann-08","exam"],["annales","The interpretation of protocol 13 - article 1 by the European Court of Human Rights is that this article:",["Prohibits the death penalty in the Member States but does not prevent a Member State from deporting an individual to a country where he/she could face the death penalty","Prohibits the death penalty in the Member States in all circumstances except in times of war and in case of treason","Prohibits the death penalty in the Member States but also prevents a Member State from deporting an individual to a country where he/she could face the death penalty","Prohibits the death penalty in the Member States but also prevents a Member State from deporting an individual to a country where he/she could face the death penalty except if permission to do so is given by the European Court of Human Rights"],2,null,"The course states plainly that ''the fact for a member State of the Council of Europe to send someone to a country where a person faces the death penalty is not allowed'', which is the basis of the interim-measures example. The answer reserving times of war and treason describes Protocol 6, not Protocol 13; and the Court grants no ''permission'' to deport, contrary to what one of the other answers suggests.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 9 ; CM Anglais, protocols and Interim measures","ann-09","exam"],["annales","The principle of ''No Punishment Without Law'' protected by article 7 of the Convention:",["Prevents a State from increasing or reducing a penalty or a sentence retrospectively","Prevents a State from enacting civil or criminal laws that would have a retroactive effect","Prevents a State from convicting someone of a crime for an act that was not a crime at the time it was committed","Prevents a State from sentencing an individual who was refused the possibility to get legal assistance during police questioning at the police station or during trial"],2,null,"Three traps. The answer extending article 7 to ''civil or criminal laws'' is wrong because article 7 concerns criminal law only. The answer saying the article prevents ''increasing or reducing'' a penalty retrospectively is wrong because a retroactive REDUCTION is permitted (rétroactivité in mitius). The answer about legal assistance during police questioning is an article 6 issue.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 10 ; CM Anglais, Article VII","ann-10","exam"],["annales","The article dealing with the ''Protection of Property'':",["Prevents a State from depriving someone of their property even if it is in the public interest","Allows a State to deprive someone of their property without any compensation if it is in the public interest","Prevents a State from depriving someone of their property, except if it is in the public interest and subject to the payment of due compensation","Allows a State to deprive someone of their property even if it is not in the public interest, subject to the payment of due compensation"],2,null,"Public interest and compensation are cumulative conditions.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 11 ; CM Anglais, Protocole 1 article 1","ann-11","exam"],["annales","Which of the following statements is true regarding the case Ireland v UK [1978]?",["The European Court of Human Rights decided the case against Ireland on the ground that the use of sensory deprivation and disorientation techniques by the British authorities against members of the IRA in places of detention in Northern Ireland was justified by the fact that the IRA was a terrorist group","The European Court of Human Rights decided the case against Ireland on the ground that its application was inadmissible as it was not possible for a Member State to bring a case against another Member State before the European Court of Human Rights","The European Court of Human Rights decided the case against the UK on the ground of the use of torture by the British authorities","The European Court of Human Rights decided the case against the UK on the ground of the use of inhuman and degrading treatment by the British authorities"],3,null,"A violation of article 3 was found against the UK, but the five techniques were classified as inhuman and degrading treatment, not as torture, as they were not considered severe enough at the time. The answer claiming the application was inadmissible because a State cannot sue another State is doubly wrong: inter-State cases are possible, and this was the first of them.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 12 ; CM Anglais, Article III","ann-12","exam"],["annales","At what stage of the proceedings should violations of human rights be pleaded?",["Only from the appeal stage of the proceedings","Only before the national Supreme Court","Before all the national Courts, including the Courts of first instance","At any time during the proceedings, including before a national Supreme Court for the first time"],2,null,"The violation must be raised from the outset and pleaded consistently at first instance, on appeal and before the highest court.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 13 ; CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity","ann-13","exam"],["annales","The European Court of Human Rights has the power to hold states accountable for a failure to respect, protect and fulfil the rights contained in the Convention. In that respect, the Court has the power:",["To find a State responsible for a violation of a Convention right and compel it to amend its Constitution to make it comply with the convention","To annul national laws or overrule a judgment issued by a national court if they are in breach of the Convention","To find a State responsible for a violation of a Convention right and order it to amend its legislation to make it comply with the convention","To fine a State to sanction it for a violation"],2,null,"The Court cannot annul a domestic law or quash a domestic judgment, and it does not fine States: just satisfaction compensates the applicant and is expressly not punitive. Amending legislation is the very definition of a general measure.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 14 ; CM Anglais, The European Court of Human Rights","ann-14","exam"],["annales","What are the remedies available against a decision by the European Court of Human Rights declaring an application inadmissible?",["A new application, lodged within a week from the decision declaring the inadmissibility","Appeal. The decision will be reviewed by the Appeal Chamber of the Court","None. A decision declaring an application inadmissible is final and cannot be challenged","Regularisation, within a week from the decision declaring the inadmissibility"],2,null,"''If the case is declared inadmissible, there is no remedy = no possibility to appeal.'' There is no such thing as an Appeal Chamber at the Court.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 15 ; CM Anglais, How do you take a case to court","ann-15","exam"],["annales","In 2000, the European Court of Human Rights held that there had been a violation of article 14 of the Convention (''Prohibition of Discrimination'') by France due to its then applicable:",["Property Law","Inheritance Law","Employment Law","Social Security Law"],1,null,"The rule under which a child born of adultery received only half the share of the other children in a succession. France amended its legislation afterwards. The case is Mazurek v France, 1 February 2000: the violation was of article 14 taken together with article 1 of Protocol No. 1. The branch of domestic law at issue is inheritance law, which is what the question asks; the Convention hook is the protection of property, which is what makes ''Property Law'' the seductive distractor.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 16 ; CM Anglais, Article XIV","ann-16","exam"],["annales","In the case Z v UK [2001], the European Court of Human Rights considered that:",["The UK had violated article 8 (right to respect for private and family life) after two soldiers were investigated and dismissed from the armed forces because of their homosexuality","The UK had violated article 3 (prohibition of torture and inhuman or degrading treatment) by failing to remove four children from their mother as soon as the relevant public authority became aware that they were exposed to terrible neglect and unacceptable emotional abuse","The UK had violated article 10 (freedom of expression) after a journalist was fined for contempt of Court for refusing to reveal the identity of a whistleblower","The UK had violated article 13 (right to an effective remedy) as the complexity of High Court procedure made self-representation ineffective in practice"],1,null,"Z v UK is the course''s illustration that article 3 may be violated by omission: the Local Authority''s failure to act over a four-year period. Note that the Court also found a violation of article 13 in this case, so the article 13 distractor is wrong on its facts, not on its article.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 17 ; CM Anglais, Article III","ann-17","exam"],["annales","In the case Open Door Counselling Ltd and Dublin Well Woman Centre v. Ireland [1992], the European Court of Human Rights considered that:",["Ireland had violated article 6 as the applicants'' right to remain silent and not to incriminate themselves following their arrest on suspicion of serious terrorist offences had not been respected","Ireland had violated article 10 (freedom of expression) after an injunction prevented the applicants from providing information on abortion services that were legally available in other countries whereas it was illegal in Ireland","Ireland had violated articles 6 and 13 as Irish law provided no effective remedy for unjustified delays in criminal proceedings","Ireland had violated article 6 (right to a fair trial) as a woman subjected to violence by her husband was effectively denied access to a court, being unable to obtain a judicial separation for lack of financial resources and legal aid"],1,null,"The Court held the injunction disproportionate to the aims pursued: Ireland could limit the information but not prevent it altogether. Note that the answer about the woman unable to obtain a judicial separation for lack of legal aid describes Airey v Ireland [1979], an article 6 case.",3,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 18 ; CM Anglais, Article X","ann-18","exam"],["annales","An application that would be lodged by someone who was not the victim of an alleged violation of the Convention:",["Would be admissible if the Convention article that was allegedly violated deals with an absolute right, as any violation of such a right is so serious that it must be sanctioned irrespective of who is the applicant","Would be inadmissible","Would be referred to the relevant national court","Would be dismissed on the merits"],1,null,"The applicant must be directly and personally the victim. Watch the distinction with the answer saying the application would be ''dismissed on the merits'': the Court would not reach the merits at all, it would declare the application inadmissible.",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 19 ; CM Anglais, conditions","ann-19","exam"],["annales","When the deadline for an application before the European Court of Human Rights falls on a Saturday, a Sunday or a Public Holiday:",["The deadline is not extended","The deadline is extended by 12 hours on the next working day","The deadline is extended to the next whole working day","The deadline is extended to the next whole working day only if the alleged violation deals with an absolute right"],0,null,"''This period ends on the last day of that period, even if that day falls on a Saturday, a Sunday, or a public holiday (so not the same as in France).''",2,null,null,"QCM ANGLAIS, Question 20 ; CM Anglais, The time limit","ann-20","exam"],["traps","What is the difference between article 6 and article 13 of the Convention?",["Article 6 is an absolute right and article 13 a qualified one","Article 6 guarantees a fair trial before a court, whereas article 13 guarantees an effective remedy before a national authority, which need not be a court","Article 6 applies in criminal cases and article 13 in civil cases","Article 6 applies before national courts and article 13 before the European Court"],1,null,"Chahal concerned an advisory panel, Tsfayo a Housing Benefits Review Board: neither was a court, yet both raised the independence issue.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Articles VI and XIII","trp-001","concept"],["traps","What is the difference between article 13 and article 35 of the Convention?",["They are two formulations of the same rule","Article 13 applies to individuals and article 35 to States","Article 13 concerns civil remedies and article 35 criminal remedies","Article 13 creates a substantive right to an effective remedy at national level, whereas article 35 makes the exhaustion of domestic remedies a condition of admissibility before the Court"],3,null,"One is a right that can itself be violated, the other is a procedural filter.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Substantive aspects","trp-002","concept"],["traps","Which of these two statements about the death penalty is correct?",["Article 2 of the Convention abolishes it, the protocols merely confirming this","Both protocols abolish it in all circumstances, Protocol 13 merely restating Protocol 6","Protocol 6 abolishes it with exceptions (war, treason), Protocol 13 abolishes it in all circumstances","Protocol 6 abolishes it in all circumstances, Protocol 13 abolishes it with exceptions"],2,null,"6 comes before 13 in number, but 13 is the stricter of the two. The course adds treason to the Protocol 6 exceptions and cites Turkey as a non-ratifying State: see the flag.",3,"Two inaccuracies carried over from the course. Article 2 of Protocol 6 allows the death penalty only for acts committed in time of war or of imminent threat of war, not for treason. And Turkey did ratify Protocol 13, in 2006.",null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","trp-003","concept"],["traps","An application is declared inadmissible. What does that mean?",["The Court has referred the case back to the national courts","The Court has suspended examination pending the exhaustion of domestic remedies","The Court will not examine the merits at all, even if the complaint might well have been justified","The Court has examined the merits and found no violation"],2,null,"The lecturer''s example: someone convicted without a lawyer or interpreter who applies directly to Strasbourg may be perfectly right on the substance and still lose, because the case is inadmissible.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, How do you take a case to court","trp-004","concept"],["traps","Which of the following is NOT a power of the European Court of Human Rights?",["Finding a State responsible for a violation","Annulling a national law that breaches the Convention","Ordering a State to amend its legislation","Awarding just satisfaction to the victim"],1,null,"The Court neither annuls domestic laws nor quashes domestic judgments, and it does not fine States.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The European Court of Human Rights ; QCM ANGLAIS Q14","trp-005","concept"],["traps","Which of these pairs is correctly matched?",["Specific measures = measures ordered by the Court; general measures = measures ordered by the Committee of Ministers","Specific measures = compensation to the individual victim; general measures = legislative change","Specific measures = legislative change; general measures = compensation to the individual victim","Specific measures = interim measures; general measures = just satisfaction"],1,null,"The single most examinable pair in the procedural part of the course.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, When the court makes a judgement ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 5.","trp-006","concept"],["traps","Which of the following correctly distinguishes torture from inhuman or degrading treatment under article 3?",["The difference lies in whether the treatment was inflicted by a State agent or by a private individual","The difference lies in whether the treatment was intentional","The difference lies in whether the victim suffered lasting physical injury","The difference is one of degree, assessed by the Court through a severity test"],3,null,"Ireland v UK is the illustration: a violation of article 3 was found, but the threshold of torture was not reached.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article III","trp-007","concept"],["traps","An applicant complains that a public authority interfered with an absolute right, but argues that the interference was proportionate. How should the Court reason?",["The Court refers the question back to the national courts","No proportionality analysis is available: where a right is absolute, any interference amounts to a violation","The Court applies the margin of appreciation to determine whether the interference was acceptable","The Court applies the proportionality test as it would for a qualified right"],1,null,"This is the structural trap around which several annales questions are built (Q6, Q7, Q8).",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of proportionality ; Plan V.A","trp-008","concept"],["traps","Which of the following statements about inter-State cases is correct?",["A State may bring a case against another State; such cases are rare but usually important","A State may bring a case against another State only where its own nationals are the victims","A State may bring a case against another State only with the authorisation of the Committee of Ministers","A State may never bring a case against another State before the Court"],0,null,"Ireland v UK [1978] was the first, and it concerned treatment inflicted on IRA members, not on Irish nationals as such.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 2.","trp-009","concept"],["traps","Which article was violated in Johnston v Ireland, and which was not?",["Neither article was violated","Article 8 was violated (in respect of the daughter); article 12 was not","Both articles 8 and 12 were violated","Article 12 was violated; article 8 was not"],1,null,"The right to marry does not include a right to divorce, hence no violation of article 12; but Irish law failed to respect the family life of a child born outside marriage, hence a violation of article 8.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 12","trp-010","concept"],["traps","In which of the following cases did the Court find NO violation?",["Chahal v UK [1996]","MS v Sweden [1997]","Siliadin v France [2005]","Kontrová v Slovakia [2007]"],1,null,"MS v Sweden (no violation, proportionate transfer of medical data) and Kononov v Latvia (no violation of article 7) are the two ''no violation'' outcomes in the course, alongside the article 12 limb of Johnston and the Grand Chamber''s ruling in Lautsi.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais and CM ANGLAIS(1), case law","trp-011","concept"],["traps","Which case is correctly matched with its article?",["Kokkinakis v Greece / article 9","Airey v Ireland / article 11","Kontrová v Slovakia / article 6","Open Door and Well Woman v Ireland / article 9"],0,null,"Kokkinakis is the first case in which the Court found a violation of article 9. Kontrová is article 2, Airey article 6, Open Door article 10. Careful with Chahal, which the course classifies under article 13 although the article 3 risk is what the advisory panel failed to examine independently, and with Airey, where the Court also found a violation of article 8.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), case law","trp-012","concept"],["traps","Which of these case-to-article pairings is correct?",["Dokic v Bosnia and Herzegovina / article 8","Öneryildiz v Turkey / article 2","Wilson and NUJ v UK / article 10","Connors v UK / article 11"],1,null,"Connors is article 8, Wilson is article 11, Dokic is Protocol 1 article 1.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), case law","trp-013","concept"],["traps","Which case is correctly matched with its subject matter?",["DG v Ireland / failure of the police to act on reports of domestic violence","Lautsi v Italy / crucifixes in State school classrooms and the margin of appreciation","Kontrová v Slovakia / detention of a minor in a young offenders'' institution","Larkos v Cyprus / transfer of confidential medical data"],1,null,"Kontrová is the domestic violence case, DG v Ireland the detention of a minor, Larkos v Cyprus the difference in security of tenure between public and private tenants.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), case law","trp-014","concept"],["traps","Which of the following describes the correct sequence of steps before applying to the European Court of Human Rights?",["Lodge an application with the Registry as soon as the first-instance court has ruled, then continue the domestic proceedings in parallel","Raise the Convention violation substantively at first instance, plead it consistently on appeal and before the highest court, then lodge a complete application with the Registry within the time limit","Raise the violation for the first time before the highest national court, then apply to the Registry","Apply to the Committee of Ministers, which will refer the case to the Court"],1,null,"Subsidiarity, consistency and exhaustion are the three elements running through the whole procedural part.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Principle of subsidiarity ; Plan VI","trp-015","concept"],["traps","An applicant sends a complete application to the Registry by e-mail on the last day of the time limit. What is the consequence?",["The time limit is not interrupted: an application sent by e-mail or fax does not constitute a valid lodging","The time limit is interrupted, provided the original is posted within a week","The time limit is interrupted, since only the completeness of the application matters","The time limit is extended to the next working day"],0,null,"The course is explicit on this point, and the requirement is receipt by the Registry rather than dispatch.",3,"As taught: e-mail and fax do not constitute valid lodging. On the dispatch-versus-receipt question, see the flag on the time-limit questions: the Rules of Court now look to the postmark.",null,"CM Anglais, The time limit ; Plan VI.C","trp-016","concept"],["traps","Which of the following statements about the margin of appreciation is FALSE?",["It is wide on matters of taxation","Its extent depends on the nature of the issue before the Court","It is narrow on matters involving family life and discrimination","It allows a State to violate a Convention right where there is no European consensus on the question"],3,null,"The course states expressly that the margin ''does not permit a violation of rights''. It governs how a State fulfils its obligations, not whether it may breach them.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Margin of appreciation ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Section 5","trp-017","concept"],["traps","Which of the following statements about article 14 is FALSE?",["Protocol 12 would create a general prohibition of discrimination","The list of prohibited grounds is open-ended thanks to the words ''or other status''","Article 14 may be invoked on its own, independently of any other Convention right","The applicant must show less favourable treatment than others in relation to the right at issue"],2,null,"Article 14 is not a standalone right; it is precisely the limitation that Protocol 12 was designed to overcome.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIV ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 14","trp-018","concept"],["traps","Which of the following is a correct statement about the Convention''s territorial scope?",["Convention rights apply only within the land borders of the State party","Convention rights apply only to nationals of States parties, wherever they may be","Convention rights apply outside the State''s territory only where that State has made a special declaration","Convention rights may apply even where a State operates outside its own territory, wherever the activity is carried out under the control of that State"],3,null,"The glossary definition of jurisdiction and Hirsi Jamaa v Italy point in the same direction.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Glossary and Article 1","trp-019","concept"],["traps","In the lecturer''s worked example, an undocumented foreign national who has lived in France for twenty years and has three French children is served with a deportation order. How should the article 8 analysis run?",["The deportation pursues no legitimate aim, since the person has family ties in France","The deportation pursues a legitimate aim (combating illegal immigration), but the interference with private and family life is disproportionate on these facts","Article 8 cannot be invoked by a person unlawfully present on the territory","The interference is lawful and therefore no proportionality assessment is required"],1,null,"Compare with the FlixBus variant: a person stopped at the border after five minutes in France has no private or family life there to be affected, so deportation is proportionate. The legitimate aim is constant; proportionality is what varies.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, The nature of rights ; Plan III","trp-020","concept"],["traps","Which of the following statements about the rights contained in the protocols is correct?",["They bind the States that have signed the protocol, whether or not they have ratified it","They bind every Member State of the Council of Europe without distinction","They bind only the States that have incorporated them into domestic law","They bind only the States that have ratified the protocol in question, whereas the Convention rights bind all States parties"],3,null,"A protocol binds only its ratifying States, which is why the abolition of the death penalty in all circumstances does not reach a State that has not ratified Protocol 13.",2,"The course uses Turkey as its example of a State that has not ratified Protocol 13. Turkey ratified it in 2006; Azerbaijan is the correct current example.",null,"CM Anglais, protocols ; Plan II.B","trp-021","concept"],["traps","A judgment is delivered against Turkey concerning the absence of a lawyer during police custody. What conclusion does the course draw for French law?",["The judgment is irrelevant in France, as it was not delivered against France","Since French legislation was the same, the same violation would be found against France, which is why France changed its legislation","France would have to wait for a judgment delivered against it before amending its law","The judgment binds French courts directly and quashes convictions already handed down"],1,null,"This is the practical reason for relying on case law concerning other States, especially those with a similar legal system.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article VI and Principle of subsidiarity","trp-022","concept"],["traps","Which of the following is NOT a consequence of the compensatory, non-punitive function of just satisfaction?",["A claim may be declared inadmissible where the applicant suffered no significant damage","The award is equivalent to damages for the injured party","The Court may increase the award to deter future violations by the same State","Legal persons may be compensated for damage to their image"],2,null,"Just satisfaction is expressly said to compensate applicants, not to punish Member States, so there is no room for a deterrent uplift.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Just satisfaction ; Plan VI.F","trp-023","concept"],["traps","Which of the following statements about interim measures is FALSE?",["They are granted only in exceptional cases, mainly concerning extradition and deportation","Interim measures are mandatory for the State concerned","Where a request for interim measures is dismissed, the applicant may appeal against that refusal","Where the request is dismissed, the applicant must tell the Court whether the substantive application will continue"],2,null,"No appeal lies against the refusal of interim measures.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Interim measures ; Plan VI.E","trp-024","concept"],["traps","Which of the following best describes the relationship between article 3 and extradition or deportation, as illustrated by the course?",["Article 3 may be derogated from in extradition cases involving terrorism","A State may extradite a person facing such a risk provided it obtains assurances from the receiving State","A State may not send a person to a country where they would face a real risk of treatment contrary to article 3, which is why interim measures are typically sought in removal cases","Article 3 concerns only treatment inflicted on the territory of the respondent State"],2,null,"The Chahal scenario and the Texas death penalty example both rest on this logic. The course''s Texas example shows precisely that assurances given by the receiving authorities may not be trusted.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIII and Interim measures","trp-025","concept"],["traps","Which statement about the Convention and the European Union is correct according to the course?",["They are entirely separate systems: the Convention belongs to the Council of Europe and is enforced in Strasbourg, while the EU protects rights through its Charter, enforced in Luxembourg","The European Union Charter of Fundamental Rights is enforced by the European Court of Human Rights","The Council of Europe and the European Union share a single court","The Convention is an instrument of the European Union enforced by the Court of Justice"],0,null,"The two organisations cooperate but are entirely separate in structure and working methods.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Section 1 ; Plan I.B","trp-026","concept"],["traps","Under article 15, which condition must be satisfied for a derogation to be valid?",["The derogation must be authorised in advance by the European Court of Human Rights","There must be a real public emergency threatening the life of the nation, and the State must set out which rights it intends to limit and what oversight mechanisms will monitor the limitations","The derogation must be approved by a two-thirds majority of the Committee of Ministers","The derogation must be limited to a maximum period of six months"],1,null,"Derogating measures must also be strictly related to the emergency and must not contradict other rules of international law.",3,null,null,"CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 15","trp-027","concept"],["traps","An applicant complains only of a breach of national law, unconnected to any Convention right. The application will be:",["Referred to the Court of Justice of the European Union","Examined on the merits and dismissed","Inadmissible, since the complaint can only be about rights contained in the Convention","Admissible, provided domestic remedies have been exhausted"],2,null,"Ratione materiae: the Court is not a fourth-instance court for domestic law.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, conditions ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 2.","trp-028","concept"],["traps","Which is the correct order of formations, from the lightest to the heaviest?",["Committee of three, Chamber of five, Grand Chamber of eleven, plenary Court of forty-six","Single judge, Chamber of five, committee of seven, Grand Chamber of fifteen","Committee of three, single judge, Chamber of seven, Grand Chamber of seventeen","Single judge, committee of three, Chamber of seven, Grand Chamber of seventeen"],3,null,"1, 3, 7, 17: a sequence worth memorising as such.",2,null,null,"CM Anglais, Composition of the Court ; CM ANGLAIS(1), 4.","trp-029","concept"],["traps","Which of the following is the best summary of what the applicant must prove to succeed under article 13?",["That no effective remedy was available before a national authority independent of the body said to have violated the right, in conjunction with an arguable complaint under another Convention right","That the applicant was denied legal aid in the domestic proceedings","That the domestic courts wrongly dismissed the complaint on the merits","That the domestic proceedings lasted more than a reasonable time"],0,null,"Chahal is the model: the article 13 violation lay in the absence of an independent examination of the article 3 risk.",3,null,null,"CM Anglais, Article XIII ; CM ANGLAIS(1), Article 13","trp-030","concept"]]'::jsonb) x;
